    {
      "number": 889,
      "title": "W2-141: Now + Available work views — research, design, and the liveness fix it proved",
      "body": "Closes W2-141 (research + product design). The design doc lived in `scratch/` and is cleared at submit per the repo rule, so the durable findings are recorded here and in the three follow-on Linear tasks.\n\n## The code change\n\n`Liveness::probe` called `command_exists(\"tmux\")`, which runs `tmux --version`. tmux only accepts `-V` and exits 1 on the long flag, so the probe reported tmux as **absent on every machine** — pinning liveness to `Unknowable`, short-circuiting `is_gone()` to false, and making the attention rule *\"process is gone but the Session still records 'running'\"* dead code that had never fired anywhere.\n\nMeanwhile `process_alive` was correct all along — it's computed by `tmux_session_exists()` via `has-session`. Two liveness paths in one snapshot disagreed: one could see the process, the other believed tmux wasn't installed.\n\nNow it asks PATH. `command_exists` is untouched — its five other callers probe `gh`, which does accept `--version`.\n\n**Dogfooded before/after:** `product-performance` held a Project Session recording `running` with `process_alive: false` and raised no attention. It now reads `process is gone but the Session still records 'running'`. Across both waves the registry claimed 5 active sessions; only 2 tmux processes existed.\n\n## The design (research → decisions)\n\n**`lf status <wave> --json` already returns the whole join** — Wave → Projects (KRs + Project Session runtime) → Tasks (PM item + Task Session runtime + worktree + branch + PR + next-move owner), plus `attention` and `runs` in `Evidence` wrappers. The PM↔Session join is already done by id (`waves.rs:643-646`). So this was never a \"what data do we need\" problem.\n\n**Availability is the absence of a Session, not a PM property.** Any surface computing it from the planning store alone must reconcile against runs afterward — by *title matching*, per the docstring on the unused `RegistryQuery.backlog()`. That's the join that rots. The predicate is `!completed && runtime == None`, and both halves come from one read.\n\n**One query, two shapes.** A lens may change the *shape*, not just filter one outline:\n- **Now** — flat, cross-wave, grouped by attention (Ready for review / Needs input / Working / Stopped / Failed). This is where Claude Code's agent view landed, and Conductor — the healthiest worktree manager — uses a status-grouped list, not lanes.\n- **Roadmap** — the `Wave → Project → Task` tree from the PM snapshot. Complete, and renderable with **zero processes running**.\n\n**The row carries three orthogonal facts, never collapsed into one word:** PM completion × Session status × process liveness. Against real Product data the cross-product lands in six cells, and three are only legible as a pair — notably `done × none` (3 real rows: W2-87, W2-126, W2-114), which is \"closed outside Loopflow\", neither an error nor identical to a merged Session.\n\n**A dead process is not a dead task.** Ghost sessions aren't an error state to clean up; they're `Stopped` rows that stay attachable and resumable and say why they stopped.\n\n**No second store.** Now/Available stay client-side predicates. Obsidian Bases states the thesis: *\"There is no `from` or `source`… the filters section narrows the dataset\"* — the view holds the question, the store holds the answer. Dataview is the counter-case: a second index buys speed, constrains the query language, then goes stale.\n\n**No kanban.** The board metaphor is losing empirically (Vibe Kanban sunsetting, Crystal deprecated, Terragon shut down), and Vibe Kanban's own docs admit dragging a card *\"won't trigger any functionality\"* — a board that isn't a control surface is a lie.\n\n**Zero new verbs.** `run` / `attach` / `steer` / `resume` / `diff` / `abandon` all exist. Launch is offered exactly where `session == none` — the same predicate that defines Available. Launch and availability are the same fact.\n\n## Follow-on tasks filed\n\n- **loopflow-api** — `lf work --json`: the one machine-wide read (envelope over the existing per-wave snapshot) + a derived status category, so CLI/Mac/iOS don't each re-implement a nine-arm match. Linear, Jira and Shortcut all converged independently on splitting status *name* from a fixed status *category*.\n- **loopflow-api** — `task_sessions.issue_id` is `UNIQUE`, so **one Task gets one Session, ever**. A merged or abandoned Task can never be re-run. That's the W2-140 bootstrap deadlock one level down, and it means the roadmap will render terminal rows with no legal action.\n- **mac-surface-ux** — the Now and Roadmap surfaces over `lf work`; retires the per-wave `pm show` fan-out (one spawn, not N) and deletes the unused `backlog()` / `BacklogItem`.\n\nPlus the two that already existed: **W2-139** (truthful liveness — the Now lens is only as honest as this) and **W2-131** (all-Wave roadmap, launch/attach).\n\n## Open question, not decided here\n\n**Backlog decay.** 18 of 26 Product tasks are filed-and-unstarted, and this design makes the pile *more* visible. Linear manages backlogs by decay (auto-archive); Basecamp deletes the concept; Height automated the pruning and shut down. That's a wave-level judgment about how Product carries intent, not a view predicate — picking one silently inside a rendering task would be the wrong place. Design assumes: render everything filed, no hidden staleness filter.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --lib engine::process` — 4 passed, including a new test pinning the probe to agree with whether tmux actually runs. The bug survived because nothing asserted it.\n- `cargo fmt` + `cargo clippy --all-targets` clean.\n- Rebuilt `lf` and re-ran `lf status product --json`: attention went 4 → 5 items, the new one being the ghost.\n\n**Note:** 6 `journal`/`doctor` tests fail in this worktree, but they fail **identically on the base commit with my source reverted** — pre-existing, not from this change. Left alone rather than folding an unrelated fix into this PR.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 26,
      "deletions": 2,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 894,
      "title": "project sessions: create successors after terminal pursuit",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo nextest run -p loopflow\n```\n\nOn a copy of a registry with an abandoned Project Session, run `lf project run <project>`. The old Session remains queryable, one pinned successor is created, and `lf project status <session-id> --json` shows `predecessor_session_id` / `successor_session_id`.\n\nVerified with clippy, 1,184 Rust tests, the end-to-end smoke suite, and a backup of the live registry.\n\n## Intent\n\nFix W2-140: the documented recovery path said to abandon an unsafe legacy Project Session and pursue the same Linear Project again, but the schema permanently reserved that Project id. Terminal pursuit now becomes append-only history while exactly one non-terminal successor owns current work. Task and Project launch races converge through the same partial unique index.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- `0.11.002` follows the landed task-PR migration and rebuilds only `project_sessions`.\n- The migration runner already disables foreign-key actions and validates the rebuilt graph before commit; tests prove Project events survive the rebuild.\n- Project lookups resolve the newest Session. An explicit `ps_...` id reads history, and status exposes both neighboring identities.\n- Failed, blocked, waiting, and running Sessions remain current; only completed or abandoned Sessions open a successor.\n\n## Not included\n\nNo Task multi-PR behavior changes and no automatic relaunch of unsafe legacy provider history.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 318,
      "deletions": 56,
      "changed_files": 10
    },
    {
      "number": 895,
      "title": "Wave Home: a user-owned execution address + the shared control path",
      "body": "## W2-88 — Wave Home: a user-owned execution address + the shared control path\n\nA Wave's **Home** is a user-owned execution address — an *owner* plus a\n*location* — that every surface resolves, probes, starts, and routes through one\nshared Loopflow API. Not a host alias, not a local/remote boolean, not an lfd\ndaemon or a new Concerto protocol.\n\n### Address model — `engine/wave_home.rs`\n`WaveHome { owner, location }`. Canonical `jack@local` and `ssh://jack@host[:port]`;\nshorthand `jack@host` normalizes to the SSH URI. DNS, IPv4, bracketed IPv6, and\noptional ports flow through one `parse` funnel and one `Display` formatter. The\nowner is **required** (no implicit current-user Home) and distinct from\ncredentials, which keep riding SSH + Doppler. Reachability is never enforced at\nparse time — public IP, private/Tailscale DNS, and public host are the same\nlocation; whether it answers is operational evidence.\n\n### Control path — `ops/home.rs`\n- `probe_home` resolves a Wave→Home and classifies **Unreachable | Stopped |\n  Running | Unknown** with a reason and, when running, the attach endpoint. Local\n  reads the endpoint directly; remote routes `lf status --json` through the Home's\n  SSH address (`capture_routed`).\n- `start_home` is **idempotent**: it probes first and returns a running\n  resident's identity instead of launching twice; a reachable-but-stopped Home is\n  started **on the Home** (local: detached tmux `lf wave`; remote: the same over\n  SSH), then re-probed for the attach identity.\n- Everything routes through the Home address and the existing SSH/credential\n  machinery — no surface reimplements SSH or owns a parallel lifecycle.\n\n### Evidence on the shared DTO\n`WaveSnapshot.home` carries the address (owner + structured location);\n`WaveDetailSnapshot.home_runtime` (`HomeRuntimeDto`) carries state + reason +\nattach endpoint + the one contextual `HomeAction` — **Attach** when running,\n**Start on `<home>`** when stopped, the reason otherwise. `lf status <wave>`\nprobes the focused Wave; `lf ls` stays address-only (no per-wave SSH). Swift\nmirrors the DTOs (`RegistryQuery.swift`) and the fixture round-trips.\n\n### CLI / transport / routing\n- `lf home probe <wave>` and `lf home start <wave>` (`--json`) — the shared\n  surface a conductor calls without implementing SSH.\n- `ssh` transport carries `owner@host` + `-p port`; `capture_routed` feeds the probe.\n- Repo/PR/release/PM commands in a remote-home Wave forward over `lf ssh`;\n  `LF_HOME_ROUTED` breaks the forward loop. Project/Task launches inherit the\n  Home via `LF_WAVE_HOME`.\n- `docs/wave-authoring.md`: Home frontmatter + `lf home` usage.\n\n### Verified\n- Unit: address parse/format for every form; owner-required rejection; IPv6\n  bracketing; probe classification (running/stopped/null-registry/garbage→unknown);\n  start session naming; DTO round-trips; routed preamble + `-p`; action-follows-state.\n- End-to-end with the built binary: `owner@host` routing forwards to the SSH\n  destination (bounded fast-fail); local Home runs in-process; `lf home probe`\n  prints `jack@local [stopped] → Start on jack@local` and the matching `--json`.\n- `cargo fmt`/`clippy` clean; touched-module tests green (6 pre-existing lib\n  failures are a shared-store DB-version mismatch, unrelated).\n\n### Downstream — Mac Now / Roadmap (not in this PR)\n- **Conductor UI**: the Open/Attach/Start button, Home navigation, showing a Wave\n  at `jack@local` / `ssh://jack@host`. The shared contract is delivered; the\n  pixels consume it. Replace `MacLocalWaveAgentLauncher`'s tmux lifecycle with\n  `lf home start`.\n- **Remote attach transport**: the SSH tunnel to a remote resident's loopback\n  endpoint (the shared API returns the identity; the UI owns the tunnel).\n- **Remote cold-start live coverage**: the remote detached `lf wave` launch is\n  constructed and unit-tested but not yet verified against a live host (Cron-host\n  bootstrap earns that).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1489,
      "deletions": 31,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 896,
      "title": "lf cron sync: install a wave's repo-owned schedules as launchd jobs",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf cron sync --wave infrastructure   # reconcile launchd jobs to match GOAL.md crons:\nlf cron list                         # prove what's installed\n\n# bring a host up end-to-end (bounded lf ssh, secret-free, idempotent)\nscripts/bootstrap-cron-host.sh mini-heart infrastructure\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nStands up a maintained Mac mini cron host (mini-heart) that runs `lf` on a schedule via launchd — no daemon or resident wave listener required. Schedules are declared once in the wave's `wave/<wave>/GOAL.md` frontmatter (`crons:`), and `lf cron sync` reconciles the host's launchd jobs to match that declaration: it installs (or replaces) a job per declared cron, prunes jobs for the wave whose flow is no longer declared, and reports schedules launchd can't run. Running it twice against the same declaration is idempotent.\n\nlaunchd's `StartCalendarInterval` only expresses a repeating wall-clock time, so `sync` accepts only fixed-daily schedules: it parses the cron expression with the `cron` crate, takes two consecutive fires, and requires them exactly 24h apart. Sub-daily, weekly, and multi-time expressions are skipped with a reason rather than silently mismapped — the resident listener still honors the full expression.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `lf cron sync --wave <wave>` command reconciling launchd jobs against `GOAL.md` `crons:`.\n- `sync_crons`, `schedule_from_cron`, and `daily_time_of` in `ops/cron.rs`, with `CronSyncResult`/`SkippedCron` result types; `Schedule::Daily` becomes `Schedule::DailyAt { hour, minute }` so the plist renders the declared time.\n- `release/CRON_HOST.md` documents the host, prerequisites, repo-owned schedules, and v0 limitations (host-local timezone, daily-only shape).\n- `scripts/bootstrap-cron-host.sh` probes reachability/auth, checks `lf` + Doppler, syncs, and lists — idempotent and secret-free.\n- Tests cover daily-time extraction, non-daily rejection, install/prune reconciliation, and skip-on-unschedulable.\n- Provisioning against mini-heart is blocked until the host answers on the tailnet; the repo-committable half is done and verified locally (see `scratch/questions.md`).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 392,
      "deletions": 9,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 897,
      "title": "wave stream: delta-granular live-turn wire",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf thread follow          # watch a wave's live turns grow, delta by delta\n```\n\nThe stream reconstructs each open turn from a whole-`turn` frame plus a run of small `turn-delta` increments, so the wave's speech renders token-by-token without a whole turn crossing the wire per token.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe live-turn wire re-broadcast the entire accumulated `ChatTurn` on every content delta — O(prose²) bytes over a turn. This replaces that with delta-granular frames: a whole `turn` frame opens (and re-baselines) a turn at the O(1) events — opening, body-session update, finalization — and each in-turn content increment rides a small `turn-delta` frame carrying just the new `ConversationItem`. Clients absorb each delta into their reconstructed open turn with the same fold the listener uses, so the reconstruction matches the served turn exactly. When the stream lags, a `resync` frame drops the reconstruction and the reconnect's whole-turn replay rebuilds it cleanly. The wire now carries O(fragment) per token.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `TurnDelta` DTO (`turn_id` + `item`) with a round-trip fixture under `tests/fixtures/dto/`, mirrored in Swift (`ChatTurn`/`WaveChatClient`)\n- Runtime broadcasts a `TurnBroadcast` enum (`Whole`/`Delta`) over a single channel so frame order and the lag counter stay unified; frames stay `Arc`-shared for one serialization across N subscribers\n- `lf thread follow` reconstructs open turns from `turn`/`turn-delta`/`resync` frames and breaks the connection on `resync` to trigger a fresh replay\n- Server emits delta frames on content increments and heals gaps with `resync`\n- `follow`/`stream_events` callbacks return `ControlFlow` so a reader can break the stream\n- Tests cover delta growth, tool-item routing, resync-drops-reconstruction, wire amplification, and the updated `wave_stream` bench for the broadcast enum",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 703,
      "deletions": 90,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 898,
      "title": "supervision: BodyObservation state model + projection (W2-135 PR1)",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow child_session\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA Session is durable intent; a body is the disposable process acting for it. This PR gives that distinction a type: `BodyObservation` records what the *current body* is observed doing (Working, Stalled, Recovering, NeedsInput, Stopped, Failed, Terminal, Unobservable), separate from the Session's durable `status`. The observation is *derived*, never stored — a pure `observe()` projection over durable intent, whether the body is alive, and how long since it last made meaningful progress. No second monitor store.\n\nLiveness (a body exists) and progress (it advanced) are separate inputs, and `Working` vs `Stalled` is exactly their difference. Terminal intent dominates every signal, and an unobservable body is never asserted gone. Each category carries its evidence, the owner who must act next (Session/Loopflow/Human/Nobody/Unknown), and the legal controls a surface may offer — one shape every surface (CLI, Mac, iOS, chat, Now/Roadmap) reads.\n\nThis is PR1 of W2-135: the read model only. The precise per-flow progress deadline arrives with the write lease in a later PR; until then `DEFAULT_STALL_AFTER` (30 min) is the honest coarse bound the projection can prove.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `child_session`: `BodyCategory`, `BodyOwner`, `BodyControl`, `BodyObservation`, `BodyIntent`, `BodyEvidence`, and the clock-free `observe()` projection, with unit tests covering the Working/Stalled threshold, unobservable, stopped-not-gone, and terminal/failed/waiting rows\n- `ProjectSessionStatus` and `TaskSessionStatus` each gain `body_intent()`, coarsening durable status onto the shared `BodyIntent` so one projection serves Project and Task Sessions alike",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 373,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 899,
      "title": "pm: give every wave its own Linear team and Task prefix (1/2 — binding + init)",
      "body": "First of two serial PRs for W2-155. This one is the **forward-cut**: bind each\nwave to its own Linear team so new work gets wave-scoped identifiers\n(PRD-*, INF-*, INT-*) instead of the shared W2-*. Existing issues are untouched.\n\n## Why this works\nA Task's identifier prefix is derived by Linear from the **team** its issue\nlives in — nothing in loopflow generates `W2`. Every wave shared one\nmachine-global team (`config.linear.team`), so everything was W2-*. Reads follow\nInitiative → Project → Issue and are team-agnostic, so binding a new team never\ndisturbs a wave's existing issues; the team only steers *creation*.\n\n## What's here\n- `pm.linear_team` in GOAL.md frontmatter — the stable team **id** (identity);\n  the key (`PRD`) stays mutable presentation.\n- `lf pm init --team-key <KEY>` adopts or creates the wave's team, **diagnosing**\n  a key owned by another team or a name reused under a different key instead of\n  guessing. Idempotent: a fully-bound wave is a no-op with zero network touch;\n  an initiative-linked wave (today's state) just gains a team.\n- `build_client` resolves the wave's team, falling back to `config.linear.team`\n  for unbound waves (no regression for anything not yet migrated).\n- `lf pm doctor` flags waves missing a team binding and any two waves sharing a\n  team.\n\n## Tests\nUnit: `ensure_team` adopt/create/conflict paths (mock Linear), GOAL round-trip\nfor `linear_team`, `resolve_team` wave-over-config precedence, default key\nderivation, and the new `--team-key`/`--team-name` parse. `cargo clippy --tests`\nclean.\n\n## Not here (PR 2/2)\nThe `lf pm reteam` migration that moves existing settled issues to per-wave\nteams, protecting active Sessions and preserving completed W2 history. See\n`scratch/give-every-wave-its-own.md`.\n\n## Deliberate deferral\nThe live proof (real PRD-/INF-/INT- task creation) is a human rollout step —\nrunning `lf pm init` creates real Linear teams, and doing it mid-Task on the\nlive product wave could interfere. The wiring is unit-proven; the rollout is a\ndeliberate external action.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 484,
      "deletions": 46,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
