      "number": 900,
      "title": "lf roadmap: machine-wide intent view and Task reference contract",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf roadmap                 # planned work across every Wave on this machine\nlf roadmap --wave product  # scope the same snapshot to one Wave\nlf roadmap --json          # wire snapshot for Loopflow (Mac/iOS)\nlf status product          # live execution + attention for one Wave\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds `lf roadmap`, the machine-wide intent plane: every Wave's durable local plan joined to whatever live Task/Project execution evidence exists, bucketed into attention sections (`now`, `needs_attention`, `available`, `later`). Sections are derived once in Rust and stamped on the wire so CLI, Mac, and iOS bucket identically without re-deriving the rule. Alongside the command, the builtin skills that report on more than one Task now share a single reference contract, and `task_clarify` gains a computable design contract so a Task's finish line is an observable outcome, not a pile of implementation receipts.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`lf roadmap` command** — new `RoadmapSnapshot` / `WaveRoadmap` / `RoadmapProject` / `RoadmapSection` wire types (required-or-Optional, no serde defaults), wired through `lf.rs` and `waves.rs`. Reuses the leaf snapshots `lf status` already emits.\n- **Shared Task reference** — `wave_pursue`, `project_pursue`, `scan`, `assess`, and `wave-report` skills all render Tasks as `[identifier](provider URL) — active PR/workspace slug — status/next owner`, sourced strictly from roadmap/status JSON with no reconstructed URLs, branches, or slugs.\n- **Computable design contract** — `task_clarify` requires user-visible outcome, end-to-end proof, source of truth, affected surfaces, absent/error states, operational boundary, and exclusions before leaving clarify.\n- **`TaskReferenceSnapshot` / `TaskWorkspaceSnapshot`** — stable issue-URL + workspace evidence that outlives a Task's process and final PR; moved worktree/branch off the runtime snapshot.\n- **`tmux_live_sessions`** — batched `list-sessions` lookup so callers checking many sessions pay one fork instead of one `has-session` per name.\n- **PM `url` field** on `PmItem`, plumbed through Linear and the ops output tests.\n- **Swift + DTO fixtures** — `WaveWorkMap`, `RegistryQuery`, task/wave detail views updated; new `roadmap_snapshot.json` fixture and `wave_detail.json` changes with matching fixture tests.\n- **README** — documents the `lf status` / `lf roadmap` / `lf pm` split.\n\nDesign note under `scratch/make-lf-work-the-machine.md`.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1504,
      "deletions": 117,
      "changed_files": 23
    },
    {
      "number": 901,
      "title": "sessions: lease provider bodies across handoffs",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task resume INF-123 --model codex --reason \"Claude quota exhausted\"\nlf project resume <project-slug> --model codex --reason \"Claude quota exhausted\"\n```\n\nPlain `resume` continues the same provider transcript. `--model` keeps the Task or Project Session, its directive, worktree, and active PR chain, but hands the next body generation to another agent. It refuses while another body is still writing — interrupt that body first.\n\n## Summary\n\nA durable Session and the provider process running it are now cleanly separated. The provider process plus its transcript is a replaceable \"body generation\"; the Session — identity, directive, worktree, supervision state, PR history — persists across handoffs. This lets a Task or Project survive provider failure or a rate limit by resuming under a different agent without losing durable work, instead of being pinned to the model it started on.\n\n`ChildProcessGeneration` is reframed as a write lease: `generation` is a monotonically increasing fencing token, and only the current generation may act for the Session, so a replacement body advances the token rather than assuming the old body's identity.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `lf task resume` and `lf project resume` gain `--model` and `--reason` (reason requires model); the ops layer threads the handoff request through to the store.\n- New `ChildBodyHandoffRequest` and `ChildBodyHandoff` types; `TaskEventKind::BodyHandedOff` / `ProjectEventKind::BodyHandedOff` emit typed audit events surfaced in chat activity.\n- Session status display shows `body:` (generation + agent + provider) in place of a bare provider string.\n- SQLite child-session store reworked to persist and advance body generations across handoffs.\n- Docs (README, `docs/lf.md`, `docs/waves.md`, troubleshooting, flowloop README) updated to describe bodies as replaceable generations and point rate-limit recovery at `resume --model`.\n- Minor: `InboxAction::Deliver` now boxes its `InboxItem` to shrink the enum.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 922,
      "deletions": 142,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 902,
      "title": "pm: lf pm reteam — migrate existing issues into per-wave teams",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf pm reteam --wave designer            # dry run: names every issue it would move, defer, and leave\nlf pm reteam --wave designer --apply    # move the settled open issues into the wave's team\n```\n\n`lf pm doctor` flags issues still stranded in the old team, and `lf pm sync` surfaces the same as a diagnostic.\n\n## Summary\n\nOnce a wave is bound to its own Linear team, its older issues still live in the shared team. `lf pm reteam` migrates them. It **defaults to a dry run** — printing every issue it would move (with the renumbering, e.g. `W2-155` → `PRD-<next>`), every one it defers, the completed issues it leaves as historical, and the target team — and only mutates with `--apply`.\n\nThe move is safe because Linear keeps the stable issue UUID across a team change, so Session/PR/comment links survive even though the number is reassigned. Reteam is conservative and idempotent: it defers any issue whose Task has a non-terminal Session (running, waiting, blocked, failed, …) to protect live or in-review work, leaves completed issues in the shared team as historical (shipped `W2-*` references are immutable, so renumbering buys nothing), and skips issues already carrying the target team key. Each moved issue gets a comment recording its prior identifier.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `PmCommand::Reteam { wave, apply }` subcommand wired through `run_pm`, with a dry-run-by-default result printer.\n- `pm_reteam` in `ops/pm.rs`: classifies each issue (move / defer / historical / already), executes moves via a new `move_item_to_team` Linear mutation, comments the old identifier, and refreshes the snapshot.\n- Pure `classify_reteam_item` / `identifier_has_team_prefix` helpers, unit-tested for the move/defer/leave/skip matrix and prefix-collision guard.\n- `lf pm sync`/`doctor` now report open issues stranded outside a team-bound wave's team as reteam candidates.\n- README documents the command and its safety guarantees.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 526,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 903,
      "title": "sessions: fence child bodies with explicit write leases",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task status W2-135 --json \\\n  | jq '.latest_process | {generation, agent, provider, state, outcome, tmux_name, pid, process_group_id}'\n```\n\nThe generation receipt now exposes the current provider, lease state, terminal outcome, tmux/PID, and owned process group for a Task or Project Session — while the write-lease token stays private to the runner and never surfaces in status or events.\n\n## Summary\n\nOnly one body may write for a durable child Session (Task or Project) at a time. This PR makes that ownership explicit with a per-generation write lease: the runner mints an opaque `ChildLeaseToken` plus generation number, hands it to the provider body via env (`LF_TASK_LEASE_TOKEN`/`LF_TASK_GENERATION` and the Project equivalents), and every store write that mutates a child body — appending events, delivering/accepting/failing commands, reconciling stale deliveries — is now gated on validating that lease. A stale body whose lease has been revoked or superseded can no longer clobber the record. The lease token implements neither `Serialize` nor `Display`, and its `Debug` is redacted, so an error path can't leak it into logs, events, or status.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `ChildWriteLease` / `ChildLeaseToken` capability types plus `ChildLeaseState` (legacy/reserved/active/revoked/finished) and `ChildBodyOutcome` (completed/interrupted/failed/lost/superseded/legacy-stopped).\n- `ChildProcessGeneration` receipt gains `agent`, `provider`, `provider_session_id`, `process_group_id`, `state`, and `outcome`; runners record the observed provider and reap the owned process group.\n- `ChildTarget` carries its lease; `apply_input` and interrupts validate the lease before touching the harness, and store command mutations move to `*_for_lease` variants.\n- New `BodyLeaseChanged` Task/Project event rendered into chat activity.\n- Migration `0.11.003_child_body_lease.sql` and expanded sqlite store logic to persist and validate leases.\n- `docs/waves.md` documents the generation receipt and the private write-lease token.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3945,
      "deletions": 559,
      "changed_files": 20
    },
    {
      "number": 904,
      "title": "mac: add all-wave roadmap controls",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```text\nOpen Loopflow → All Repos → Roadmap\nStart, attach, resume, or interrupt a Task from its roadmap row.\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMake the default Wave Chat pane consume the shared `lf roadmap --json` snapshot across every registered Wave. Durable plan rows remain visible for stopped Waves, per-Wave read failures retain their evidence, and whole-query refresh failures keep the last successful roadmap on screen.\n\n## Changes\n\n- render the canonical Wave → Project → Task hierarchy with its server-derived sections\n- route Wave open/start and Task run/attach/resume/interrupt controls through existing lifecycle commands\n- reuse the Task workspace for agent attachment, diffs, files, shells, PRs, and worktree opening\n- cover roadmap action selection and exact lifecycle argv with the shared DTO fixture\n\n## Verification\n\n- `swift test -Xswiftc -gnone` — 103 tests\n- `uv run python ../scripts/check_swift_multiplatform_boundaries.py`\n- macOS `xcodebuild build-for-testing`\n- live `lf roadmap --json` read across seven live/stopped Waves, including one explicit unavailable plan",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 782,
      "deletions": 42,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 905,
      "title": "gate: bound every phase and split off the required UI host gate",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/test.py --all         # bounded full gate; prints elapsed / budget per phase\nuv run python scripts/test.py --list --all  # the plan and every phase budget, run nothing\nuv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host     # the required hosted LoopflowUITests run (permissioned host)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMakes `scripts/test.py --all` a bounded and honest local release gate. Every phase now runs under a printed wall-clock budget (`PHASE_BUDGETS`); an overrun is killed—process group and all—and reported as `TIMEOUT <phase> (budget Ns)`, so no phase can hang the gate. The summary states what each suite actually proves, closing the gap where the `loopflow` suite looked like it exercised UI behavior when it only compiles the app and UI-test runners. The real hosted `LoopflowUITests` run is split off into a separately named **required** gate (`--ui-host`) that never runs under `--all` because it needs macOS UI-automation permission; a missing permission is reported as a named `MISSING CAPABILITY` failure with a next action, never a silent skip. On failure, phase logs and any `.xcresult` are preserved under `.lf/tmp/gate/run-<pid>/<suite>/` for one-command repair without opening Xcode.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Per-phase wall-clock budgets with SIGTERM→SIGKILL group-kill on overrun; unlisted phases fall back to `DEFAULT_BUDGET_S`.\n- New `ui-host` required gate: runs `xcodebuild test -only-testing:LoopflowUITests`, with a precheck that fails fast on non-macOS or a simulated capability gap (`LF_UI_HOST_SIMULATE_NO_PERMISSION`), and a classifier that treats runner-bootstrap failures as capability gaps while keeping genuine assertion failures raw.\n- `Suite` gains `proves`, `host_gate`, `precheck`, and `classify` so the summary never over-claims.\n- `release/GATE_BUDGET.md` documents the budgets and a headless CI measurement (run 29396456085); `release/UI_HOST_GATE.md` documents the maintained host, the permission it needs, and how absence is reported.\n- `test_gate_bounded.py` covers timeout-kill, group-kill, actionable failures, missing tools, `--all` never running the host gate, and the capability-classification paths.\n- `TESTING.md` and a CI comment cross-reference the two-level UI split and the budget doc.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 567,
      "deletions": 29,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 906,
      "title": "runs: separate skill runs from process execs",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf runs\nlf status --wave core\nlf execs\nlf trace <exec-id>\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMake runs mean agent-backed skill invocations, where context, model, token, cost, and outcome evidence belongs. Wave status filters that same dataset, while process-level orchestration diagnostics move to execs and trace accepts an exec id.\n\n## Changes\n\n- add `lf execs` for the raw process ledger\n- report skill runs with context and token totals in `lf runs` and `lf status`\n- make `lf trace <exec-id>` reconstruct the containing process tree\n- mirror the run DTO in Swift and cover the split with behavior tests",
