    {
      "number": 908,
      "title": "lf store: isolate dev and release control-plane stores",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf doctor            # prints build provenance, resolved DB path, latest known + applied migrations\n\n# Development builds resolve their own per-worktree store:\n#   ~/.lf-dev/worktrees/<source-identity>/loopflow.db\n# Release builds share the production store:\n#   ~/.lf/loopflow.db\n\n# Break-glass: point a dev build at the production DB\nLF_ALLOW_PRODUCTION_DB_FROM_DEV=1 lf ...\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nBuilds now carry an embedded provenance (`development` or `release`) so a branch checkout can no longer migrate the production store or another worktree's dev store. `build.rs` stamps provenance and a source-root identity into the binary; CI release workflows set `LOOPFLOW_BUILD_PROVENANCE=release` explicitly. Development builds resolve a store under `~/.lf-dev/worktrees/<source-identity>/loopflow.db` derived from the checkout path, and refuse `~/.lf/loopflow.db` even when it arrives via `LF_DB_PATH` unless `LF_ALLOW_PRODUCTION_DB_FROM_DEV=1` is set. Session runners pin their binary, home, and store through internal `LF_CONTROL_BIN`/`LF_CONTROL_HOME`/`LF_CONTROL_DB_PATH` variables, and provider agents no longer inherit `LF_BIN`, `LF_HOME`, `LF_DB_PATH`, or the break-glass flag. Before advancing an existing on-disk database, migrations now take an exclusive lock and write an atomic backup named for the previously applied migration.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `build_info` module: embedded provenance + stable per-checkout source identity, with a `build.rs` emitter and validation\n- Store resolution splits dev vs release paths; control-plane env vars pin session binary/home/db and are scrubbed from vendor agent environments\n- Atomic pre-migration backup under an exclusive transaction (`rusqlite` `backup` feature, `fs2` file lock)\n- `lf doctor` reports provenance, resolved DB path, and latest known/applied migrations, even when the DB is too new or from a divergent dev build\n- CI release/nightly/weekly workflows stamp `LOOPFLOW_BUILD_PROVENANCE=release`\n- Test support and new `test_environment_tests` isolate storage while preserving temp-home config; docs (`architecture.md`, `lf.md`, `MIGRATIONS.md`) updated",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1488,
      "deletions": 179,
      "changed_files": 33
    },
    {
      "number": 911,
      "title": "mac: add the NOW work lens over one roadmap read",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nswift test --package-path swift --filter WorkAttentionTests\n# then launch the Mac app with every wave stopped — the default surface is NOW\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nPR 1 of W2-146: give the Mac a **NOW ⇄ ROADMAP lens** over the single `lf roadmap --json` read, so a user sees all current and available work across every wave in one surface with zero wave processes running. NOW is a flat, cross-wave list grouped by who must move next — *Ready for review, Needs input, Working, Stopped, Failed* — ordered oldest-first within each group. ROADMAP keeps the existing Wave › Project › Task tree. Both shapes are pure projections over the same snapshot; `generated_at` stays on screen so staleness is visible. This ships the headline \"see all current and available work in one surface\" with no new Rust and no wire change.\n\nWork rows carry the three orthogonal facts on independent channels — PM completion, Session status, process liveness — never collapsed into one word. A Stopped row (dead process, non-terminal Session) stays attachable/resumable and says why it stopped: a dead process is not a dead task. Ineligible rows are greyed with their reason attached, never hidden.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `WorkAttention.swift` in the shared `Loopflow` package: `nowGroup(for:)` — a pure, total bucketing function over shared roadmap fields (`section`, `next_move.owner`, `runtime.status/process_alive`, `active_pr`, `task.completed`) — plus `NowRow`/`NowSection` flattening, oldest-first ordering, empty buckets dropped. Predicate lives in the shared package so iOS/CLI can reuse it verbatim.\n- `RoadmapView`: `WorkLens { now, roadmap }` toggle, header retitled \"Work\", flat NOW rendering, `roadmapTaskIsActionable` greyed-with-reason discipline.\n- New `WorkAttentionTests` proving each Task lands in the expected group off the shared fields.\n- Deletes `RegistryQuery.backlog()`, `BacklogItem`, the `items` field on `PmShowSnapshot`, and `RegistryQueryTests.backlogDecodesOpenTasks` — availability is the absence of a Session, not `!completed` over a title-match join.\n- Design doc `scratch/w2-146.md` (removed on land).\n\nHome control (wiring `home_runtime` + `lf home start` onto the Wave row) is PR 2 and out of scope here.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 511,
      "deletions": 88,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 912,
      "title": "wt list: make inspection side-effect free",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf wt list          # inspect worktrees without touching canonical main\nlf wt list --sync   # explicitly fetch and fast-forward main first\n```\n\nRun `git status --short` before and after bare `lf wt list`: local edits, HEAD, refs, and the stash remain unchanged.\n\n## Summary\n\nMakes `lf wt list` a side-effect-free inspection command. It previously called `sync_main` unconditionally, which could fetch, hard-reset, and auto-stash the canonical checkout merely to render a list. That made a read command capable of disrupting Wave and Project control-plane work.\n\nFreshness is now explicit: bare `lf wt list` reports against the last-synced main, while `--sync` opts into fetching and fast-forwarding before the read.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add `lf wt list --sync` as the explicit mutating path.\n- Keep bare `lf wt list` read-only.\n- Document the freshness behavior in CLI help.\n- Prove bare list preserves HEAD, working tree/index, stash, and refs.\n- Prove `--sync` performs the requested fast-forward.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 111,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 913,
      "title": "pr: reconcile out-of-band merges + add lf pr next",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# A PR merged out of band (GitHub auto-merge, not `lf pr land -c`) and follow-up work remains:\nlf pr next            # reconcile the merge, rotate to the next serial PR, carry uncommitted edits forward\nlf pr next my-slug    # name the next serial branch explicitly\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAn out-of-band merge (GitHub auto-merge armed by `lf pr land`, not settled by `lf pr land -c`) used to strand a Task in a state no advertised command could repair: a stray open/draft sibling on the branch could hide the merge, and follow-up edits sat uncommitted with no clean way to rotate to the next serial PR. This PR adds three narrow, testable fixes proven by two dogfood incidents (W2-116/#905 and W2-166/#907): reconcile now prefers the merged PR over noisier siblings, `lf pr next` rotates the Task forward while carrying dirty edits, and the scratch stash moves under the ignored `.lf/tmp` prefix so it stops dirtying the worktree.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`lf pr next [slug]`** — new command that reconciles an out-of-band merge into the settled PR, then rotates the worktree to sequence N+1, carrying preserved follow-up edits forward. Refuses if the current PR isn't merged yet or the Task is terminal.\n- **Reconcile prefers the merged PR** — `select_reconcile_pr` ranks merged > open/draft > closed (newest as tiebreak), so an out-of-band merge is never hidden by a stray sibling left behind by a land.\n- **Rotation carries dirty trees on demand** — `RotateOptions { carry_dirty, slug_override }` splits the runner's strict clean-tree rotation from the operator's `lf pr next`, which carries uncommitted edits and can name the next branch.\n- **Scratch stash under ignored prefix** — moved from `.lf/scratch-stash/` to `.lf/tmp/scratch-stash/` so it no longer dirties the worktree and blocks `lf task complete`.\n- **Guidance** — task-pursue skill and runner task seed now advertise `lf pr next` for the out-of-band-merge case.\n- Design doc under `scratch/` (removed on land).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 253,
      "deletions": 13,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 914,
      "title": "stacked Tasks: place child worktrees on the active parent PR",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n    lf task run CHILD --stack-on PARENT\n\nPARENT must have an active published PR. CHILD gets its own readable sibling worktree and branch forked from that PR. `lf pr open` targets the parent automatically.\n\nThe selector binds to the active PR at launch. Later serial PRs in PARENT do not move CHILD.\n\n## Delivery\n\n- Persist the exact parent Task PR and fork commit.\n- Rebase child-only work when the parent changes.\n- Refuse child land before the parent merges.\n- After merge, collapse child-only commits onto main and retarget the PR.\n- Keep same-Task multi-PR delivery serial through `lf pr land --next` and `lf pr next`; remove `lf pr stack`.\n\n## Proof\n\n- Migration namespace check\n- Rust PR, land, and rebase integration tests\n- Clippy with warnings denied\n- Prompt golden tests",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 798,
      "deletions": 47,
      "changed_files": 35
    },
    {
      "number": 915,
      "title": "resolve: one ambient-wave resolver for every lf command",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# From a resident wave's environment (LF_WAVE_ID set to the durable UUID):\nlf status          # resolves the ambient wave\nlf pm show         # resolves the SAME wave — no longer ignores the env\nlf pm status\n\n# Explicit --wave still wins everywhere:\nlf pm show --wave product\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nEvery `lf` command that acts on \"the wave I'm inside\" now reads the ambient wave through one shared resolver instead of each consumer inventing its own rule. The bug (W2-151) was never that a resident exports `LF_WAVE_ID=<uuid>` — that's correct — but that consumers disagreed on how to read it: `status` handled a UUID, `pm show` ignored the env entirely, and others silently dropped a hand-set name. This introduces `resolve_managed_wave_name` (and a sync, store-free variant for `lf pm`) with one rule: explicit `--wave` wins, else `LF_WAVE_ID` as a durable registry UUID mapped to its wave's name, else a hand-set name used directly, else a classified error. A UUID the registry has never seen is a loud `StaleIdentity` error rather than being silently re-read as a name.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `wave_context`: new `WaveResolveError` (NoContext / StaleIdentity / UnknownExplicit / Registry) and the `resolve_managed_wave_name` async resolver plus `resolve_managed_wave_name_sync` for sync PM call sites\n- `lf pm`: every arm resolves the ambient wave through the shared helper; `NoContext` preserves the old \"all waves / pass --wave\" behavior, a stale id errors loudly\n- `lf status`: `resolve_status_wave` delegates to the shared resolver, then requires a registry row (a wave with no row has no runs to report)\n- Tests: a resolver matrix across all seven ambient environments, plus end-to-end proof that `lf status` and `lf pm show` agree from a resident wave's environment\n- Design + open questions captured under `scratch/`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 379,
      "deletions": 41,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 916,
      "title": "task ci-fix: give a waiting task's open PR a CI-derived next move",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf status <wave-id>\n```\n\nFor a task whose PR is open, the reconcile path now reads GitHub's required checks and `lf status` reports a CI-derived next move: **CI** owns it while required checks are pending or failing, then it hands back to **Review** once they go green. Failing reasons name the offending checks, e.g. `required checks failed: build, lint`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThis persists a per-head CI observation on a task's PR so a waiting Task doesn't burn a body waiting on checks it can't see. On the reconcile path, when a PR is open we capture its head SHA and read `gh pr checks --required` to classify the required checks into passing/pending/failing (a `cancel` bucket counts as failing; any failure dominates pending). That reading is stored against the head commit and cleared when the PR merges or closes. `next_move_for_task` then routes an open PR by its fresh CI reading — pending/failing → CI owner, passing → Review — and falls back to plain review waiting when CI state is unknown (no head, no required checks, or gh unavailable). This lays the groundwork for waking a waiting Task into a `ci-fix` skill.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `CiObservation`/`CiState`/`CiCheck` on `TaskPr`, persisted via migration `0.11.004_task_pr_ci_state` and the store layer\n- `PrInfo`/`GithubPr` now carry `head_sha` (`headRefOid`), threaded through create/reconcile/attach\n- `required_check_state` in `ops::pr` reads and classifies required checks; unknown state is never a hard error on reconcile\n- `lf status` surfaces the CI owner and names failing checks; new tests cover the CI routing and check classification\n- Design notes and open questions under `scratch/`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 537,
      "deletions": 28,
      "changed_files": 12
    },
    {
      "number": 917,
      "title": "gate: make --ui-host re-runnable and classify the hung-runner permission gap",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host          # runs the required hosted-UI gate\nuv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host          # ...and again — no longer dies on a stale result bundle\n\n# simulate the permission gap without Xcode\nLF_UI_HOST_SIMULATE_NO_PERMISSION=1 uv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAttempting the 5/5 real-host proof for the required `--ui-host` gate surfaced two genuine defects, both fixed here. First, `_ui_host_commands` wrote to a **fixed** `-resultBundlePath`; `xcodebuild test` exits 64 rather than overwrite an existing bundle, so every run after the first died in ~1s. The bundle is now pid-scoped under `_run_artifact_root()`, making the gate re-runnable. Second, on macOS 26 / Xcode 26 the UI-automation permission denial surfaces as a hung control session (`Timed out … initiating control session with daemon` / `The test runner hung before establishing connection`, exit 65) rather than a clean TCC denial — a signature the classifier didn't recognize, so a permission gap misreported as a red test. That signature now classifies as `MISSING CAPABILITY`, verified against the real captured log.\n\nThe 5/5 green run stays open: on `Jacks-MacBook-Pro` both real-host attempts hung before `LoopflowUITests` executed, because the host hasn't granted the runner Automation + Accessibility. That approval is an interactive TCC dialog and can't be granted headlessly, so the gate now honestly reports the gap instead of a false failure.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Per-run pid-scoped `-resultBundlePath` so `--ui-host` re-runs instead of dying on a stale bundle (exit 64)\n- Hung-control-session signature classified as `MISSING CAPABILITY`, not a red test\n- Two new tests: hung-control-session classification, and per-run (not fixed-path) result bundle\n- `release/UI_HOST_GATE.md`: documents the macOS 26 / Xcode 26 denial signature and adds a verification log with the one operator action to unblock 5/5\n- `scratch/ui-host-runs/`: real-host run logs and driver capturing whether `LoopflowUITests` actually executed",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 86,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 918,
      "title": "mac: Home control on the wave row over lf home probe/start",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nOpen the Loopflow Mac app and look at any Wave card in the roadmap. Each row now shows its Home address, a probed liveness chip, and one contextual action:\n\n- **Open** — the resident is running; attaches to its endpoint\n- **Start on `<home>`** — reachable but stopped; runs `lf home start` on the *configured* Home\n- an actionable reason — when the Home can't be reached\n\n```bash\nlf home probe <wave> --json\nlf home start <wave> --json\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Wave row's old \"Start & open\" button launched the wave locally via `LocalWaveAgentLauncher`, which was wrong for any Wave whose Home is a different machine. This replaces that button with a `WaveHomeControl` that renders the shared `HomeRuntimeDto` contract: it probes the Home once per card (on appear, keyed to the Wave — never per row and never on the 15s roadmap poll), shows liveness, and offers the single action the DTO dictates. The app never does SSH itself — `lf home probe` classifies the Home (local reads instant, remote routed over credential-forwarding) and `lf home start` idempotently starts the resident on its configured Home, returning the attach identity. `openWave` is now pure navigation; starting is the control's job.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `RegistryQuery`: add `homeProbe` and `homeStart`; make `HomeState`, `HomeAction`, `HomeRuntime` public and add the new `HomeStartResult` DTO (`wave`, `started`, `runtime`)\n- `RoadmapView`: new `WaveHomeControl` view (address + state chip + contextual action); drop the local-launch path from `openWave` and the `activeControlId` start button\n- Tests: decode a `stopped` probe into its Start action, and decode a `started` result into its running attach identity",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 193,
      "deletions": 40,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 919,
      "title": "receipt: bind curated memory facts to evidence receipts",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Bind a fact to the records that justify it (repeatable for many-to-one evidence)\nlf memory add \"workers report via the stream\" \\\n  --receipt chat_turn:turn-3 \\\n  --receipt pr:loopflow/loopflow#912@abc1234\n\n# Read facts back with their receipts as JSON\nlf memory log --json\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nCurated claims — `MEMORY.md` facts, KR proofs, executive Wave reports — are human-readable summaries that, left alone, become a second unaccountable truth. This PR introduces the `Receipt`: a small, stable pointer from one curated claim to the one canonical raw record that justifies it (a chat turn, worker report, trace turn, PM change, or PR), keyed by that record's own durable id rather than a line number or session id. Receipts survive the perturbations curation outlives — Markdown edits, branch deletion, session succession, and database migration. This first slice wires receipts through memory facts: `lf memory add --receipt kind:reference` parses and stamps evidence at the CLI boundary, the live server journals each receipt alongside its fact, and `lf memory log --json` reads the receipt-bearing view back for drilling.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `receipt` module: `Receipt` / `EvidenceKind` wire types (no serde defaults), `kind:reference` parsing with per-kind opaque references and a `run` alias for `worker_report`\n- `lf memory add` gains a repeatable `--receipt` flag; parse errors surface as user errors at authoring time\n- `lf memory log --json` emits facts with their receipts, read directly from the local journal (receipts are journaled, not carried on the live prose stream)\n- Journal `MemoryAdded` events now carry `receipts`; `append_memory` and `write_memory` thread them through\n- Swift `Receipt` model + DTO fixture (`tests/fixtures/dto/receipt.json`) keeping the Rust/Swift wire types in lockstep\n- Design doc under `scratch/` describing the receipt contract",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 563,
      "deletions": 39,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 921,
      "title": "auth: route provider OAuth accounts",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf auth connect claude --account primary\nlf auth connect claude --account reserve\nlf auth use claude primary\nlf auth accounts claude\nlf ssh mini -- lf wave product\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nRoute Claude and Codex OAuth sessions across operator-owned accounts while preserving provider session continuity, shared Loopflow skills, and process-lifetime SSH credential forwarding.\n\n## Changes\n\n- add private native provider profiles with deterministic health-aware selection, cooldowns, preferences, and session pins\n- expose account connect, list, use, enable, disable, reset, and disconnect commands\n- consume Claude and Codex rate-limit frames and restart cooled sessions on a healthy account\n- forward only locally refreshed access-token leases over SSH stdin, with no remote auth files or refresh tokens\n- preserve the existing single-account behavior when no managed accounts exist\n\n## Verification\n\n- cargo test -p loopflow\n- cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings\n- cargo fmt --all -- --check",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2811,
      "deletions": 102,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 922,
      "title": "mac: trajectory & evidence section — record and inspect wave memory",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nOpen a Wave's detail on the Mac. Below its projects, a new **Trajectory** section shows the Wave's curated memory facts oldest-to-newest, each with its evidence receipts as `kind:reference` chips (a `pr:` receipt is a clickable GitHub link). Record a new note inline:\n\n```bash\n# CLI write → Mac read round-trips within one refresh\nlf memory add \"trajectory: chose X over Y\" --receipt pr:loopflow/loopflow#918@57ca881 -w product\n\n# Mac write → CLI read: what you Record on the Mac shows up here\nlf memory log --json -w product\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis is PR 1 of the W2-86 arc — conducting a Wave end to end from the Mac. It ships the smallest coherent conduct slice: read a Wave's curated trajectory, inspect the evidence behind each note, and record a new evidence-backed note, round-tripping to the same state `lf memory` shows in a terminal. Reads are daemon-less journal folds (`lf memory log --json`), so the trajectory renders whether or not the Home is live; the write goes through the Wave's live Home (`lf memory add`), the sole holder of MEMORY.md's pen, so a stopped Home surfaces a Start-on-Home hint instead of failing silently. There's no Mac-only domain model — the section decodes the shared `MemoryFact`/`Receipt` Codable types and renders. Later serial PRs on this branch add calibration approval, beat scheduling, the `lf receipt show` drill, and iOS parity.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `Receipt.browserURL` resolves a `pr:` receipt (`owner/repo#N`, optional `@sha` pin dropped) to a GitHub pull-request URL; other kinds render token-only until the receipt drill lands.\n- `RegistryQuery` gains `memoryLog(wave:cwd:)` (daemon-less read against the wave's origin repo) and `memoryAdd(wave:fact:receipts:cwd:)` (shells `lf memory add`, surfacing the no-server error).\n- New `WaveTrajectorySection` / `TrajectoryNoteView` inside `WavePlanView` — facts list, receipt chips, compose field with a Home-liveness gate, and RoadmapView-style refresh/error idioms; polls on the plan pane's existing refresh signal.\n- DTO fixture test coverage for the receipt/memory-fact wire shape.\n- Design note under `scratch/` scoping PR 1 and naming the remaining serial slices.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 270,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 923,
      "title": "Context Lab: research and refine real session context",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n1. Select the `product` Wave and open **Context Lab** from its header.\n2. Open **Sources** and select `headless surface` or `wave_pursue`.\n3. Confirm main's current file fills the primary pane beside impression and\n   revision evidence.\n4. Choose a Refinement Project if prompted.\n5. Click **Refine in task-worker** only when ready to create a real Task.\n\n## Intent\n\nContext Lab makes the text shaping a Wave's agent sessions inspectable and\neditable. It separates initial-prompt load, lifetime provider input, and peak\nrequest pressure, then pairs those measurements with a Sources worklist ranked\nby agent-session impressions.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Context Lab opens from a selected Wave, with repo and Wave fixed for the\n  window's lifetime. It is progressive disclosure from the primary Wave UI,\n  not a global destination.\n- Selecting a source opens main's current file as the primary object;\n  historical revisions and representative trace addresses remain evidence.\n- Rust owns session reconciliation, source identity, impressions, provider\n  normalization, and coverage. Swift renders one atomic snapshot and owns\n  interaction state.\n- **Refine in task-worker** creates and starts a normal Project-owned Task and\n  opens the existing Task workspace on its Agent view. Single-Project Waves\n  route automatically; multi-Project Waves remember an explicit Refinement\n  Project.\n- The handoff refreshes the Wave plan and Context Lab snapshot, validates the\n  source receipt, then rechecks main's source immediately before Task creation.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- An impression is one distinct agent session whose captured initial prompt\n  contains the source at least once.\n- Missing provider or source-level capture stays unavailable rather than\n  becoming zero.\n- Cost remains outside Context Lab; context-window pressure is the comparable\n  capacity signal.\n\n## Not included\n\n- No embedded Markdown editor, alternate agent host, synthetic quality score,\n  or hidden Task lifecycle.\n- Automated review stops before the Task-creation click. The resulting live\n  agent session, source diff, and backlink are the human-review checkpoint.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Full repository gate passed after rebasing onto current main.\n- Python: 59 passed.\n- Rust: formatting and clippy passed; 1,418 tests passed, 2 skipped.\n- Website: 59 passed, 3 skipped.\n- Swift: 133 tests across 23 suites passed; multiplatform boundaries passed.\n- E2E smoke passed; Xcode app/UI-test `build-for-testing` passed.\n- Migration order and checksums passed through `0.11.012`;\n  `git diff --check` passed.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 7092,
      "deletions": 332,
      "changed_files": 36
    },
    {
      "number": 924,
      "title": "Task PR range: prove M==B parity + guard placement at the source",
      "body": "## What\n\nGuarantees every Task PR contains only that Task's work (W2-138), on two fronts:\n\n**Publication proof (backstop).** `verify_task_pr_range` gates `ops/land.rs::prepare_pr` before the first GitHub side effect. The invariant is `M == B` where `M = merge-base(origin/<default>, HEAD)` and `B` = the recorded `TaskPr.base_commit`:\n- `M == B` — GitHub range == `lf task changes`; publish.\n- `M < B` — recorded base carries commits absent from origin (the #877/#882 contamination); **refuse before push**, naming the foreign commits/files and the `git rebase --onto` recovery.\n- `B < M` — stale/squash-merged base; heal `base_commit → M` (dedicated `heal_task_pr_base` store write, since base_commit is optimistic-identity in the generic update).\n- divergent — refuse as ambiguous.\nNo remote falls back to local `<default>`.\n\n**Placement guard (source).** `resolve_upstream_base` anchors placement + serial rotation on fetched `origin/<default>` (explicit local fallback with no remote); `refuse_if_canonical_ahead` stops `task_run` when canonical main is ahead of its upstream — the root cause of #877/#882.\n\n## Proof\n\n10 unit tests over all five Proof scenarios (parity, contamination-refuse, stale-heal, squash-merged-parent-heal, no-remote, rotated continuation) plus placement (origin-anchor, no-remote fallback, ahead-of-origin refusal, in-sync allow). land/pr/worktree integration suites green.\n\nConsumes `TaskPr {base_commit, sequence, branch}` — no new stack/PR-state model (W2-93/W2-172 boundary).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 445,
      "deletions": 14,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 925,
      "title": "revert mac trajectory and evidence section",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nBuild and open the Mac app, then select a Wave. The Trajectory section introduced by #922 is absent; existing Wave detail, Now, Roadmap, and Home controls remain unchanged.\n\n## Summary\n\nReverts #922 in full. The trajectory-and-evidence interaction was not the desired Mac conduct model, so this removes that product direction cleanly before further W2-86 work builds on it. The broader Task remains open for redesign.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Remove the Wave Trajectory section and note composer.\n- Remove the branch-specific memory log/add query helpers.\n- Remove PR-receipt browser URL projection added only for that surface.\n- Remove the corresponding DTO fixture assertions.\n\nVerification: swift test --package-path swift passes all 108 tests.",
