    },
    {
      "number": 926,
      "title": "auth: trust Claude browser callbacks",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf auth connect claude --account primary\n```\n\nAuthorize in the browser; no paste-code prompt appears, and the command completes from Claude callback confirmation.\n\n## Summary\n\nStop treating UUIDs in Claude OAuth URLs as device codes and make auth subprocess ownership cancellation-safe.\n\n## Changes\n\n- parse user codes only from explicit URL fields or code-prompt text\n- cover the real Claude callback URL shape with a regression test\n- terminate the provider process group when auth is cancelled or Loopflow exits\n\n## Verification\n\n- cargo test -p loopflow\n- cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings\n- cargo fmt --all -- --check",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 170,
      "deletions": 21,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 928,
      "title": "task: preserve local control through GitHub PR observation failures",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task status <issue>          # shows PR observation freshness\nlf task follow-up <issue> \"...\" # keeps working during a GitHub outage\nlf pr next [slug]               # rotates to the next serial PR, carrying follow-up forward\n```\n\nDuring a GitHub outage or an exhausted API quota, every Task control command that triggers PR reconcile (`status`, `follow-up`, `steer`, `interrupt`) used to fail outright, because reconcile enumerated remote PRs with `gh pr list` and propagated any transport error. GitHub is a reconciliation input, not the Task's store of record — this PR makes that true in the code. Reconcile now reads the one persisted `publication.github` number through a single bounded `gh api repos/:nwo/pulls/:n` REST call; an unpublished working PR has no number and is not read remotely at all. Quota, network, and gh failures return a degraded observation that preserves the cached `TaskPr` row and records a named reason on the session instead of erroring, and `lf task status` surfaces the reason and retry boundary.\n\nAlongside the observation change, two adjacent ways a Task could lose work are closed. Serial PR rotation now carries both committed post-merge follow-up (the range between GitHub's recorded merged tip and the branch tip) and dirty working-tree edits onto the next branch, rolling back cleanly to the settled branch on a cherry-pick conflict so a failed rotation is retry-safe rather than half-applied. And a merged PR no longer auto-completes the Task while an accepted directive is unincorporated — an auto-merge armed by `lf pr land` must not silently erase direction accepted after it was armed; the Task waits and says why.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `ops::pr::observe_pr_by_number` replaces `current_or_merged_pr_for_branch` / `select_reconcile_pr`: reading the exact persisted number can't be fooled by a stray draft sibling, and it still returns `head_sha` for the CI-check reading. Failures classify into named reasons (rate limit, network, 404-as-deleted-ref).\n- New `GithubObservation` on `TaskPr` (migration `0.11.019`) coalesces reads across short-lived `lf` processes: a 60s TTL after success, a 5 minute circuit after a failure, so an outage isn't hammered by every command.\n- New `task::Observation` freshness view (`NotRequired` / `Fresh` / `Cached` / `Degraded`) on the session snapshot and control result, printed by `lf task status` and `lf task control`.\n- `task_stack` resolves stack parents through the same cached, outage-tolerant reconcile instead of enumerating PRs on its own.\n- Git helpers: `cherry_pick_range` (aborts on conflict, leaving HEAD untouched), `stash_including_untracked`, `stash_pop`.\n- Tests cover REST state/head mapping, quota and 404 classification, degrade-and-preserve-cache, skipping the read for unpublished PRs, and the observation cache (`task_github_cache_tests.rs`).\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1283,
      "deletions": 140,
      "changed_files": 20
    },
    {
      "number": 929,
      "title": "pull-local-bin: fix empty-args expansion under macOS bash 3.2",
      "body": "bash 3.2 treats \"${args[@]}\" as unbound under set -u when the array is empty, so the script died on line 65 whenever run with no flags. Guard the expansion with ${args[@]+...}.",
