    {
      "number": 933,
      "title": "Roadmap: share Task attention across CLI and Mac",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\nRun the shared projection and fixture checks:\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow lf::commands::waves\ncd swift && swift test\n```\n\nThe Rust suite proves the eight-state attention decision table and that\n`lf roadmap` prints the shared reason verbatim. The Swift suite decodes the\nsame fixture and proves NOW membership, controls, and accessibility text.\n\nFor the full local gate:\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/test.py --all\n```\n\n## Intent\n\nGive terminal and Mac surfaces one explainable Task attention signal. Rust\nprojects PM, Session, process, PR, and local-progress evidence into green, red,\nblack, or unknown with one reason and a set of safe controls. Swift consumes\nthat contract directly instead of maintaining another lifecycle state machine.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Durable Task Sessions own workspace identity.\n- One machine-wide tmux snapshot is the process-liveness boundary.\n- An active PR's immutable base commit is the comparison point for unsettled\n  Task-authored commits.\n- Missing evidence must remain visible as unknown rather than defaulting to a\n  clean state.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Keep PM completion independent from attention.\n- Carry constituent evidence in the DTO so explanation UI never reruns Git.\n- Derive legal lifecycle controls in Rust and share them with every consumer.\n- Bound Git work to one cleanliness probe and, for an active PR, one HEAD read\n  per Task with a durable Session.\n\n## Not included\n\nProject/Wave attention, history and Audit presentation, Project blocker\npreservation, and joining the interactive-handoff store contract into Task\nattention remain follow-on slices.\n\nThe CI-equivalent local matrix passes. Two separate hosted UI attempts were\nstopped by macOS UI-automation authentication before the test runner\ninitialized; package tests and the signed app/UI-runner build pass.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1112,
      "deletions": 84,
      "changed_files": 15
    },
    {
      "number": 934,
      "title": "wave chat: paint durable history before live refresh",
      "body": "## Summary\n- paint bounded durable Wave Chat history before endpoint discovery\n- preserve honest missing, partial, and unavailable states while reconciling live replay by stable turn ID\n- collapse exact-equivalent operational failures without removing raw journal or disclosed attempt detail\n\n## Verification\n- Rust formatting and clippy passed with warnings denied\n- Rust nextest passed 1,332 tests with 3 skipped in an isolated task environment\n- Swift passed 116 tests across 22 suites after rebasing main\n- macOS app and UI-test runner build-for-testing passed\n- Product journal returned the latest 12 readable turns as partial and truncated without mutating its incompatible tail\n- 20 process-level history reads averaged about 154 ms",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 782,
      "deletions": 55,
      "changed_files": 19
    },
    {
      "number": 935,
      "title": "handoff: persist the shared interactive session contract",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow interactive_handoff --lib\ncargo test -p loopflow --test handoff_tests\nswift test --package-path swift --filter DTOFixtureTests\n```\n\n## Intent\n\nEstablish the first W2-175 slice: one durable interactive child Session beneath a Wave, Project Session, or Task Session, backed by the machine-shared store and exposed through `lf handoff`.\n\n## Contract\n\n- Persist one unresolved handoff per parent with stable identity, Home, cwd, provider history, body generation, reason, environment, and attach argv.\n- Keep W2-135 authoritative for PID, process group, lease token, recovery, and provider process ownership.\n- Make open and attach replay-safe; preserve the first terminal outcome; expose one durable parent-wake claim.\n- Return a shared `{session_id,status,cwd,host,environment,argv}` descriptor without terminal bytes.\n- Mirror that descriptor in Swift through one round-trip fixture.\n\n## Decisions\n\nThe active handoff row is the parent waiting marker, so there is no second status machine or generic Session catalog. Terminal completion, hand-back, and failure are explicit outcomes. A later parent generation may claim the wake, but only the first claim mutates the row.\n\n## Ordered delivery\n\n1. This PR lands the shared store, CLI, lifecycle tests, and Rust/Swift descriptor contract.\n2. The next serial W2-175 PR connects deliberate Wave, Project, and Task escape points to this row, reconciles child death and restart against W2-135, and consumes the one wake so the same parent resumes once.\n3. W2-175 closes only after ten real handoffs preserve Session identity, provider history, worktree, and single-resume evidence.\n\nMac Active Sessions, embedded Ghostty, and external or remote presentation adapters remain separate consumer Tasks. They consume this descriptor rather than extending its lifecycle.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The runtime prepares the provider-backed terminal before calling `lf handoff open` in this first slice.\n- Project and Task opens must match the current W2-135 body generation and provider history.\n- A Wave handoff uses the Wave pass or resident replay boundary; it does not invent a Project or Task body lease.\n- Migration `0.11.008` was the next ordinal on this branch; an ordinal-only rebase may be needed if another migration lands first.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow interactive_handoff --lib` — 8 passed\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --test handoff_tests` — 1 passed\n- `swift test --package-path swift --filter DTOFixtureTests` — 5 passed\n- `uv run python scripts/check_migrations.py`\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`\n- `git diff --check`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1892,
      "deletions": 3,
      "changed_files": 19
    },
    {
      "number": 936,
      "title": "auth: route repositories through profiles with automated Claude browser handoff",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Register a profile bound to a Chrome profile on this host\nlf profile create jack@loopflow.studio --chrome-profile jack@loopflow.studio\n\n# Connect or import provider accounts, then attach them to profiles\nlf auth connect claude --account jack --profile jack@loopflow.studio\nlf auth import claude --account personal --profile jackstah@gmail.com\nlf auth set claude jack --login-email jack@loopflow.studio --plan max\nlf profile account set jack@loopflow.studio claude jack@loopflow.studio\n\n# Give a repository a default profile plus ordered backups\nlf profile route set \\\n  --default jack@loopflow.studio \\\n  --backup loopflow-eng@loopflow.studio \\\n  --backup jackstah@gmail.com\nlf profile route show\n\n# Forward the repo's route to a remote host\nlf ssh mini -- lf wave product\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the flat \"preferred account\" model with persisted **profiles** that route each repository through a default account plus ordered backups. A profile is a routing identity bound to a Chrome profile on the host; provider accounts (Claude/Codex) attach to profiles and keep independent auth and session state under `~/.lf/accounts/`. Shared accounts are tried once and share a single cooldown, and a provider child stays pinned to its selected profile and account for its lifetime.\n\nClaude authorization now runs through Claude in Chrome when the browser extension is connected, driving the OAuth handoff automatically and falling back to a hidden terminal code prompt when the controller is unavailable. `lf auth import` adopts an existing isolated Claude credential — or the current macOS Keychain login when the account home is empty — without a fresh OAuth flow. Account lifecycle (login email, routing mode, plan, paid-through) moves onto `lf auth set`, and `lf ssh` forwards the current repo's ordered route, resolving and refreshing each referenced credential once even when profiles share an account.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `lf profile` command group and `profile.rs`: profile creation, account attachment, and per-repo route configuration\n- New `profile` and `provider_account_lifecycle` store migrations; `ProviderAccount` gains `login_email`, `credential_state`, `routing`, and plan/paid-through fields\n- `auth connect`/`auth import` gain `--profile` and `--chrome-profile`; `auth set` replaces `enable`/`disable`/`use` for account lifecycle\n- Automated Claude browser authorization via the Chrome controller, with a `rpassword` hidden-prompt fallback for the one-time code\n- macOS-only Keychain guard (`security-framework`) that preserves and restores the host login around isolated Claude connects, cfg-gated to macOS\n- `lf ssh` forwards ordered profile routes and each credential once, with restarts inheriting the routing lease and accrued cooldowns\n- README rewritten to document profile-based routing, browser handoff, and provider-specific `paid-through` semantics",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 4638,
      "deletions": 565,
      "changed_files": 20
    },
    {
      "number": 937,
      "title": "auth: keep profile examples generic",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/demo_profile_routing.py\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplace the original operator-specific profile identities and route with `example.com` fixtures across the README, demo, and tests. Real profile identities, provider bindings, and repository route order remain local Loopflow configuration; the shipped system carries no personal account topology.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --lib`\n- `cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings`\n- `uv run ruff check scripts/demo_profile_routing.py`\n- `uv run python scripts/demo_profile_routing.py`",
