    },
    {
      "number": 940,
      "title": "Store: converge live migration history",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nConverges the one exact branch-local migration history that reached a production ledger before main established its canonical sequence. The canonical chain now includes context pressure and input normalization before the already-shipped profile/account schema.\n\nUnknown histories still fail closed. Adoption runs only when both the divergent version prefix and the resulting product schema match exactly.\n\n## Safety\n\n- preserves application timestamps while rewriting only known ledger identities\n- applies the missing interactive-handoff schema before continuing\n- publishes an unmodified pre-migration backup\n- validates every divergent prefix and retains product rows\n\n## Verification\n\n- 22 migration tests passed\n- `uv run python scripts/check_migrations.py`\n- `cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings`\n- live ledger integrity and product row counts verified before this PR",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 274,
      "deletions": 7,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 941,
      "title": "flow: make interactive wait or defer explicit",
      "body": "## Intent\n\nGive every resolved interactive flow step one explicit policy outcome: wait for a human or defer the interaction. This is the engine seam for Project/Task review points and `--skip-interactive`; the existing `next_action` behavior remains require-by-default.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- The same expanded flow produces either `WaitInteractive` or `DeferInteractive`; no twin headless flow is needed.\n- Non-interactive skills still run under the defer policy.\n- `InteractionPolicy` is the resolved two-state runtime value; authored inheritance lands at the Project/Task layer.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow interaction_policy -- --nocapture`\n- `cargo clippy -p loopflow -- -D warnings`\n- `cargo fmt --check`\n\n## Next PR\n\nPersist the resolved Task flow/policy and flow cursor, then record deferred interaction obligations and connect required steps to the durable handoff contract from #935.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 65,
      "deletions": 3,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 942,
      "title": "task: persist flow selection and resume position",
      "body": "## What changed\n\n- add --flow to Task launch and pin the resolved all-skill flow on the Task Session\n- persist the next flow cursor and iteration after each completed turn\n- reconstruct the Task playhead from durable state on every body generation\n- store the resolved interaction policy now so later defer/require behavior has one runtime value\n- expose flow, policy, and position in Task status JSON and text\n\n## Why\n\nA Task restart currently rebuilds the default task flow at step zero. Interactive review work needs a stable inner loop before required/deferred rendezvous can be wired without replaying prior work.\n\nThe --skip-interactive flag is intentionally not in this slice: it will land with obligation persistence so the flag cannot silently defer a review without recording the debt.\n\n## Verification\n\n- cargo fmt --all -- --check\n- cargo clippy -p loopflow -- -D warnings\n- cargo test -p loopflow --lib (1180 passed before the migration-tip assertion; the corrected migration convergence test passes)\n- uv run python scripts/check_migrations.py\n- git diff origin/main...HEAD --check",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 361,
      "deletions": 42,
      "changed_files": 15
    },
    {
      "number": 943,
      "title": "auth: connect managed Codex accounts with native OAuth",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n    lf auth connect codex --account engineering --profile engineering@example.com\n\nApprove the provider-owned page in the Chrome profile Loopflow opens. Codex returns through its local callback; there is no device code to copy.\n\n## Summary\n\nManaged Codex accounts now use Codex native local OAuth callback while Loopflow owns profile selection, credential isolation, identity verification, and routing. The provider continues to own sign-in and authorization UI.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Open native Codex OAuth in the selected host-local Chrome profile.\n- Read and match the authenticated email from the resulting ID token before registration.\n- Keep expired or missing native account homes out of automatic rotation.\n- Avoid printing OAuth URLs, state, or device codes.\n\n## Review\n\nThe Codex path has no Claude-in-Chrome dependency and performs no provider-page automation. The real browser callback completed successfully with an isolated mode-0600 native credential home. Full Rust tests and clippy pass.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 268,
      "deletions": 92,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 944,
      "title": "linear: stream human Linear edits into Task Sessions via webhooks",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nConfigure the Linear webhook secret through Doppler, then send issue and comment webhook deliveries to the Loopflow webhook endpoint. Human edits become durable Task direction exactly once.\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces polling with verified Linear webhooks, persists delivery and directive state transactionally, and wakes the owning Task Session without duplicate incorporation. The migration recovery regression follows the registry latest version instead of freezing legitimate future migration growth.\n\n## Verification\n\n- cargo test -p loopflow --test linear_observe_tests --test linear_webhook_tests\n- cargo test -p loopflow store::migrations::tests::live_permuted_history_converges_without_losing_rows\n- cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings\n\nLinear: W2-206",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1826,
      "deletions": 17,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 945,
      "title": "task: add kickoff iterate gate lifecycle",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow task_lifecycle --lib\ncargo test -p loopflow task_phase_epoch_allows_resets_and_rejects_stale_positions --lib\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nGive every Task one durable lifecycle: kickoff once, then iterate and gate until the gate approves its proposed outcome. Review can return the same Session, provider transcript, worktree, and serial PR history to another iterate cycle.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Pin kickoff, iterate, and gate flows plus their interaction policies on each Task.\n- Fence cursor resets with a phase epoch and preserve the exact proposed terminal outcome for gate approval.\n- Reopen material gate changes into Iterate while ignoring gate-only scratch evidence.\n- Migrate existing Tasks into Iterate so upgrades do not replay kickoff.\n\nInteractive reviewer routing, attended handoffs, `--headless`, Project flow inheritance, and manual Wave catch-up follow in stacked PRs.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 805,
      "deletions": 185,
      "changed_files": 17
    },
    {
      "number": 946,
      "title": "top: include live provider activity",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n    lf top\n\n## Summary\n\nShow provider-reported output-token activity during running Codex sessions, not only terminal run receipts. Include live lf and provider processes with their worktree names, and show the complete process list.\n\n## Changes\n\n- bucket Codex session token counters across the last hour\n- retain non-Codex ledger receipts without double-counting Codex completions\n- discover Codex, Claude, Gemini, and OpenCode processes alongside lf\n- document lf top as the machine-health check in operating guidance",
