    {
      "number": 978,
      "title": "handoff: honest surface reach + shared-attach launcher",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf handoff open --provider claude --provider-session sess_… -- claude --resume sess_…\nlf handoff attach <id> --json   # reopen returns the same session_id + argv\n```\n\nIn the app, Open runs the shared attach command — Ghostty embeds it, Warp renders it into a command-bearing launch config, and IDEs are offered as worktree-only. A remembered surface that can no longer attach falls back to Ghostty with a visible reason.\n\n```bash\nswift test --filter \"HandoffSurface|ActiveSessions\"   # 28/28\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nPR #969 landed the surface-resolution model but classified VS Code and Cursor as `.attach` while no launcher actually resumed the specific durable provider Session — the user-visible capability lied about the launch. This PR makes the label match the launch: only a surface that runs the **exact shared attach command** claims `.attach`. A new `HandoffSurfaceLauncher` runs that command per surface, the resolver reports *why* it fell back when a remembered app can no longer attach, and the census keeps an active handoff red until it reaches a terminal outcome — not merely until a human first attaches.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`HandoffSurfaceLauncher`** (new) runs the shared attach argv: Ghostty embeds it, Warp writes a command-bearing launch config that opens `warp://launch/<name>`, IDEs open the worktree only via `NSWorkspace`. A Warp config-write failure fails the launch (visible Ghostty fallback) rather than opening a bare window.\n- **`HandoffSurfaceCapability`** drops the `providerIsClaude` / `providerSessionKnown` flags. Reach is now `installedApps + workspaceProven + warpCommandBearing + isRemoteHome`. A **remote Home** (computed from `descriptor.host`) makes local worktree-only actions `.unavailable` — Ghostty and command-bearing Warp still `.attach` since the shared argv carries its own transport.\n- **Warp preserves the descriptor environment** — `warpLaunchConfigYAML` renders a sorted `env KEY=VALUE …` prefix on the exact argv, since Warp configs carry no environment field.\n- **`HandoffSurfaceMemory.recordLaunch`** records only a user-initiated, successful `.attach` launch; a worktree-only IDE open (even user-initiated) never overwrites the last attach preference.\n- **`HandoffSurfaceResolver.resolve`** returns `HandoffSurfaceResolution { surface, fallbackReason }` — \"Warp is unavailable — using the embedded terminal.\" / \"Warp can no longer attach — …\" — shown in the sheet header so a fallback is never silent.\n- **Census** keys redness on every active handoff (waiting *or* attached), so an attached-but-unresolved handoff stays red and keeps naming the human as next owner through Task → Project → Wave until it completes, hands back, or fails.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 928,
      "deletions": 154,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 979,
      "title": "resolve: route chat, radio, memory, home, and cron through the shared ambient-wave resolver",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# hand-set LF_WAVE_ID by name now resolves (used to drop silently)\nLF_WAVE_ID=product lf chat \"shipping now\"\nLF_WAVE_ID=goals lf radio \"all green\"\nLF_WAVE_ID=product lf memory show\n\n# cron --wave is now optional; falls back to the ambient wave\nlf cron add --flow status\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nEvery CLI command that reads the ambient wave from the environment now goes through the one shared `resolve_managed_wave_name` rule instead of its own ad-hoc `id.parse()`. Before this, a durable `LF_WAVE_ID=<uuid>` worked but a hand-set `LF_WAVE_ID=<name>` fell through `parse()` and got dropped silently — `lf chat`/`radio`/`memory`/`home` behaved as if there were no wave context at all. And a *stale* UUID (registry has no such row) was indistinguishable from no context, so publishes succeeded-as-drop on a wrong context. The shared resolver fixes both: a UUID maps to its registry name, a hand-set name is used directly, and a stale UUID is now a loud, classified error that names the id rather than a silent drop.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `wave_context`: `resolve_run_wave_name` and `wave_name_for_id` route through the shared resolver, so trace attribution and `lf home` honor a hand-set name; attribution errors stay non-fatal (`None` → worktree fallback).\n- `chat::resolve_target`: only consults ambient env when the target is the ambient wave — an explicit `--wave` always wins — and surfaces a stale UUID as an error instead of dropping the message.\n- `radio::ambient_wave`/`ambient_channel`: now return `Result`, so a stale `LF_WAVE_ID` is a loud error while no-context stays the exit-0 drop; `sub` follows the new signature.\n- `cron add`: `--wave` is now optional and falls back to the ambient wave via `resolve_managed_wave_name_sync`, mapping `NoContext` to the familiar \"pass --wave\" error.\n- Tests across `wave_context`, `chat`, `radio`, and `wave_resolution_tests` cover the full matrix: UUID, hand-set name, explicit override, stale error, and no-context.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 278,
      "deletions": 45,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 980,
      "title": "task: recover abandoned work on the current successor model",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task recover INF-123 --reason \"the earlier attempt was abandoned by mistake\"\nlf task resume INF-123\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`abandon` is terminal, which meant a worktree full of real work plus an open PR had no way back — the only option was starting a fresh Task and re-doing everything. `lf task recover` closes that gap by creating exactly one linked Task Session successor that adopts the abandoned attempt's worktree, workspace slug, current directive, and full serial PR history, then hands off to `resume` to start the fresh body.\n\nRecovery refuses unsafe shapes before it moves any ownership: missing, detached, mid-operation, or branch-mismatched worktrees are rejected via the existing `task_recovery_adoption` preconditions, and the store move is atomic so a failure can't leave PRs orphaned between two sessions. A non-abandoned Task is told to `resume` instead; a completed one is told to start a new Task. Retrying `recover` after a successful recovery converges on the live successor rather than forking a second one — but only when that successor demonstrably adopted the abandoned worktree, so an ordinary new attempt on a different worktree isn't misreported as recovered.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `lf task recover ISSUE [--reason ...] [--json]` command, wired through the CLI to `ops::task::task_recover`\n- `TaskSessionSnapshot` gains `predecessor_session_id` / `successor_session_id`, backed by a new `task_session_chain_neighbors` store query, so the succession chain is visible to callers\n- New atomic `recover_task_session_successor` store operation: creates the successor, carries the directive forward as version 1, and moves PR history off the terminal predecessor in one transaction\n- Successor starts clean — `Waiting`, `Kickoff` phase, no inherited process or provider session — with the reason recorded in `status_reason` for audit\n- Tests cover the refusals, the adoption of worktree/PR/directive, retry convergence, and the store-level atomicity; README documents the recover-then-resume flow",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 571,
      "deletions": 28,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 982,
      "title": "ops: route telemetry to the git-ignored .lf/tmp/ tree",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# in a clean checkout, run any read-only op\nlf rebase --plan\ngit status --porcelain   # stays empty\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nLocal ops telemetry was being appended to a tracked path (`.lf/metrics/ops.jsonl`), so every read-only operation — `rebase --plan`, status, dispatch — dirtied the worktree. A dirty checkout then made those same ops refuse to run, breaking `lf task run` on the dogfood loop. This routes telemetry under the git-ignored `.lf/tmp/metrics/ops.jsonl` instead, so recording a metric never touches a tracked file. The stale committed `.lf/metrics/ops.jsonl` is deleted.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `ops_metrics_path()` helper as the single source of truth for the telemetry path, now under `.lf/tmp/`\n- `record_ops_metric()` writes through that helper\n- Regression test: recording telemetry into a clean checkout leaves it clean while still landing the record on disk (telemetry isn't silently disabled)\n- Removed the previously tracked `.lf/metrics/ops.jsonl`\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 74,
      "deletions": 17,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 983,
      "title": "wave surface: prove the five states render distinctly in ci",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nswift build --package-path swift --product LoopflowMac\nscripts/prove_wave_surface_states.sh          # 5 states × 2 widths, pairwise distinct\nscripts/prove_product_wave_surface.sh           # real Product data through the registry\nuv run python scripts/generate_screenshots.py   # narrow + wide captures per state\n```\n\nThe CI job `swift-test` now runs `prove_wave_surface_states.sh` after the build, so a regression that collapses two states (e.g. loading rendering as selected) fails CI by name.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe redesigned Wave surface has five Proof states — selected, loading, error, empty, and future-child indentation — and the Task (W2-178) requires them to render distinctly and stay selectable without clipping at narrow and wide desktop widths. This PR makes that proof CI-runnable without UI-automation permission: the app renders its own key window to a PNG via `SnapshotService` (`cacheDisplay`, no Screen Recording or Automation permission needed), and a script asserts all captures are pairwise distinct. A `live` app-test mode runs the same surface through the real `lf` registry read path so a missing Product snapshot fails by name instead of silently falling back to mocks.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `AppTestMode` gains a `live` mode (real registry path, keeps the test knobs) plus env readers for `LOOPFLOW_UI_TEST_WIDTH`, `LOOPFLOW_UI_TEST_DELAY`, and `LOOPFLOW_UI_TEST_SELECT_BRANCH`\n- `LoopflowApp` applies a pinned window width and a self-snapshot-and-terminate path during UI-test runs\n- `scripts/prove_wave_surface_states.sh` — 5 states × 2 widths, asserts pairwise distinct PNGs; complements the permissioned XCUITest\n- `scripts/prove_product_wave_surface.sh` — captures the real Product Wave through the production registry path; absent PM snapshot is a named failure\n- `screenshots.yaml` splits each Wave-surface state into narrow (900) and wide (1440) captures\n- New `WaveSurfaceStateTests`, `WaveSurfacePrimaryHierarchyTests`, and `WaveSurfaceLaunch` UI tests assert each state's affordance and row hittability\n- Strips registry vocabulary from the default Roadmap pane; adds accessibility identifiers for the control surface and active-sessions tab\n- Forwards `LOOPFLOW_UI_TEST_DETAIL_STATE` through to the app (the PR #972 forwarding gap that made loading/error render as selected)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 689,
      "deletions": 42,
      "changed_files": 19
    },
    {
      "number": 984,
      "title": "receipt: match PR receipts by repo+number+sha and surface inaccessible evidence",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf doctor            # receipts check now reports inaccessible vs orphaned\nlf receipt resolve pr:loopflow/loopflow#912@abc123\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe receipt sweep and `pr:` resolution now match a PR by its full identity — repo, number, and sha — instead of a bare PR number. A number that collides across repos no longer resolves the wrong PR, and a stale-sha claim is distinguished from a missing PR. The doctor `receipts` check also gains an **inaccessible** state: when the backing store or a journal can't be read this run (locked DB, unreadable wave list, bad PM snapshot), receipts of that evidence kind are surfaced with the read error rather than silently counted orphaned — a dropped read never masquerades as a dangling pointer.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `PrReference::parse` + `PrIdentity` replace `parse_pr_number`; matching keys on repo + number, with optional sha.\n- Doctor receipt sweep tracks `unavailable` kinds and per-kind read errors; new `Resolution` enum (Resolved / Orphaned / Inaccessible) drives the detail line, which now also reports `inaccessible` counts and `unreadable evidence`.\n- `gather_receipt_audit` marks a kind inaccessible on any store/journal read failure instead of judging its receipts against an empty set; a wave-list failure short-circuits chat-turn and PM kinds.\n- `resolve_pr` gives a targeted error when a PR exists under the same repo+number but a different sha (head moved / wrong commit).\n- Receipts check warns even with zero facts when there's an unreadable-evidence error, and no longer reports a clean \"nothing to audit\".\n- Docs and tests updated to cover repo-scoped matching, wrong-repo rejection, and inaccessible-vs-orphaned surfacing.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 668,
      "deletions": 83,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 985,
      "title": "sessions: derive the body observation onto the runtime snapshot wire",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf waves show <wave> --json | jq '.projects[].details[].runtime.observation'\ncargo test -p loopflow\nswift test\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis is PR4 of W2-135 (leased, progress-aware, recoverable Session bodies). The earlier PRs built the `observe()` projection and lease mechanics, but nothing on the wire ever carried the result — the runtime snapshot still shipped only `{status, process_alive}`, so no surface could tell a working body from a stalled one. This PR wires the body observation onto both runtime snapshots and mirrors it into Swift, backed by a real progress signal drawn from the durable event log.\n\nDurable intent (`status`) and the observed body state stay separate evidence: a live body that advanced recently reads `Working`; a live body silent past its progress deadline reads `Stalled`, hands ownership to Loopflow, and offers `Extend`/`Interrupt`/`Stop`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Progress signal**: new `latest_task_event_at` / `latest_project_event_at` store methods (`SELECT MAX(created_at)` over the event logs). Events append without bumping `updated_at`, so this is the honest \"has the body actually made progress\" measure — a body silent in its event log past the deadline is the >4h-sleep incident.\n- **Producers**: `progress_age()` in `waves.rs` measures seconds since the freshest of the last event and last status change (clamped at zero); both runtime producers now build `BodyEvidence`, call `observe()`, and attach `observation` to the snapshot.\n- **Swift mirror**: `BodyObservation` plus `BodyCategory`/`BodyOwner`/`BodyControl` enums in `WaveWorkMap.swift`, with `observation` decoded on both runtime snapshots.\n- **Fixtures**: coherent `observation` on every runtime block across the four DTO fixtures and the Mac mock; Rust and Swift tests pin Working / Stalled / NeedsInput on the wire.\n\nPR5 (next) drives recovery from this observation — a body that reads Stalled past its deadline is revoked, reaped, and restarted gen+1 on the same Session — plus a deterministic dogfood stall path.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 240,
      "deletions": 36,
      "changed_files": 13
    },
    {
      "number": 986,
      "title": "sessions: resolve resumed bodies through current Home lf",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow current_home_binary_never_resolves_through_the_control_pin\ncargo test -p loopflow launch_task_process_ignores_control_bin_and_resolves_current_home\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nDurable Project and Task Sessions no longer pin the lf executable, database path, or LF_HOME that launched their first body. Every resume, supervisor wake, and handoff wake resolves the current Home at launch time, while each generation records the binary that actually booted it. This keeps otherwise healthy Sessions resumable across Loopflow upgrades and migration changes.\n\nTracks [W2-225](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/W2-225/resolve-session-bodies-through-the-current-home-lf).\n\n## Changes\n\n- Stop reading and writing historical ChildExecutionContext values from Session domain state\n- Resolve the current Home executable and store before reserving a new generation\n- Stamp immutable build provenance when the child activates its lease\n- Add migration 0.11.018 for Task and Project body provenance\n- Cover current-vs-control selection, failed reservation recovery, provenance round trips, and Task/Project launch paths\n\nW2-225 stays open after merge for the published-release A-to-B recovery proof against the stranded W2-177, W2-178, and W2-224 Sessions.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 881,
      "deletions": 248,
      "changed_files": 17
    },
    {
      "number": 987,
      "title": "release: v0.11.3",
      "body": "# v0.11.3\n\nv0.11.3 makes durable Sessions survive Loopflow upgrades, tightens recovery and PR authority, and keeps the operating surfaces honest.\n\n## Sessions resume through the installed Loopflow\n\nProject and Task Sessions no longer pin the executable, database path, or `LF_HOME` from their first body. Every resume, supervisor wake, and handoff resolves the current Home at launch time, while each body generation records the binary provenance that actually booted it. This is the migration-fire repair: install v0.11.3 before resuming Sessions stranded on an older binary (#986).\n\nThe release adds migration `0.11.018_session_body_provenance`. Only an official release install should advance the production store.\n\n- Body observations now reach the shared runtime wire, so surfaces can distinguish Working, Stalled, NeedsInput, Stopped, and Failed without re-deriving lifecycle state (#985).\n- Explicit resume reaps dead Task and Project leases even when durable state is Waiting, Failed, or Blocked (#968, #973).\n- Interactive handoff Open resolves to the last successful attach-capable surface, with an embedded fallback when that surface is gone (#969).\n\n## Operations fail more honestly\n\n- Task PR publication proves and heals its authoritative range end to end before GitHub receives a PR (#977).\n- Read-only ops telemetry writes under ignored `.lf/tmp/` state instead of dirtying the checkout that dispatch depends on (#982).\n- Ambient Wave resolution is shared by chat, radio, memory, Home, cron, and run attribution; stale UUIDs fail loudly instead of dropping work (#979).\n- Creating PM work now fails closed until the Wave has an explicit Linear team binding (#975).\n- Codex bodies launched by Loopflow default to standard speed; Fast remains an explicit interactive opt-in (#976).\n\n## Evidence and Mac surfaces\n\n- Receipt resolution keys PR evidence by repository, number, and SHA, and distinguishes inaccessible evidence from orphaned evidence (#971, #984).\n- The Mac Wave/Project/Task lens and its loading, error, empty, selected, and child-indentation states are driven by shared contracts and offline proof fixtures (#963, #972).\n\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 47,
      "deletions": 92,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 989,
      "title": "task: make ambiguous ancestry refusal actionable and fully proved",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow task_pr_range\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a Task branch's recorded base and the canonical branch have diverged with no common lineage, `verify_task_pr_range` refused with a message that named neither what diverged nor how to recover — the user had to reconstruct both sides by hand. The refusal now names the commits and files on each side (`M..B` and `B..M`) and prints the exact `git rebase --onto` that recovers, all still before any push or `gh pr` call.\n\nAlongside that, the proof matrix for W2-255 extends past the single contamination case that was covered: divergent ancestry, a squash-merged parent (PR2 cut from PR1's pre-squash tip), a repo with no remote at all, and serial rotation now each have a test — at the unit level against `verify_task_pr_range_with_authority`, and end-to-end through the real `submit`/`land` path over a bare-origin fixture.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Divergence refusal in `ops::task` enumerates base-side and upstream-side commits plus affected files, and ends with the recovery command\n- End-to-end tests assert the branch never reaches the remote and no `gh pr create/edit/ready` is issued before a refusal\n- Squash-merged-parent and no-remote cases proved at both unit and integration level\n- Design notes under `scratch/`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 504,
      "deletions": 6,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 990,
      "title": "wave chat: make journal delivery failure atomic",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow wave::server\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA journal append that failed used to be logged and swallowed: the runtime still minted the message id, committed the turn to the transcript, queued it as pending, and broadcast it live. The wave looked like it had accepted the message, but on restart the journal had no record of it — the live view and restart truth silently diverged, and the sender got a 200 for a message that no longer existed.\n\nAppends now persist before anything projects them. `Journal::try_append` serializes, records the file's byte length, writes, and flushes — and only then advances the sequence and narrates. If the write or flush fails, the file is truncated back to the checkpoint, so no torn tail is left behind and no id is burned; the same runtime can retry the same message. `Journal::append` keeps the old infallible shape for the runtime paths that can't report failure, but it now panics rather than continuing on a split brain.\n\nThe HTTP door takes the fallible path. `WaveRuntime::try_deliver` returns the append error, and `messages_handler` maps it to a 500 with `message was not accepted`, leaving the transcript, pending queue, and turn/inbox broadcasts untouched. A retry of the identical POST then succeeds and lands as `turn-1`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `JournalAppendError` carrying the seq and the stage that failed (serialization, file inspection, write, flush, or rollback), with rollback failure folded into the message\n- `try_append` / `try_deliver` / `try_deliver_message` fallible variants; `append`, `deliver`, and `deliver_say` expect on them\n- Test-only failure injection (`fail_next_journal_append`) at the write and flush stages, gated behind `#[cfg(test)]`\n- Server test proves both failure stages are rejected with nothing observable left behind, that a retry is accepted, and that the message survives reopening the wave",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 245,
      "deletions": 26,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 991,
      "title": "resolve: preserve stale ambient wave identity in trace and run attribution",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# point at a wave id the registry has no row for\nLF_WAVE_ID=$(uuidgen) lf design\n# warns: ambient wave identity failed validation; run attributed to no wave\n#        — pass --wave <name> to recover\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA stale ambient `LF_WAVE_ID` used to disappear: `resolve_run_wave_name()` collapsed every resolver error to `None`, and callers then inferred a wave from the worktree name. The run got attributed to whatever wave the worktree happened to look like, and the stale identity left no trace — so the wrong attribution looked identical to a legitimate no-context run.\n\nThis PR introduces `run_attribution()`, one classification shared by the `lf` run wrapper and `journal::ensure_run_context`. It separates the three cases the old `.ok()` flattened together: a valid UUID or hand-set name attributes to that wave; absent context attributes to no wave with no failure (worktree inference stays legitimate there alone); a stale UUID or registry read error attributes to no wave *and* records a classified failure naming the stale source and the `--wave <name>` recovery. Attribution stays non-fatal — the run proceeds, but it records `wave: None` honestly and carries the failure on the existing `error` field so the stale source stays visible and actionable (W2-239).\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `RunAttribution` and `run_attribution()` in `engine::wave_context`; `resolve_run_wave_name()` is now a thin wrapper for non-attribution callers (`lf home`).\n- `lf.rs` warns on a failed ambient identity and emits both wave and failure on the run-started event; `ensure_run_context` debug-logs the same and stops falling back to the worktree.\n- Tests cover the classification arms (absent context, hand-set name, unregistered name) and an end-to-end check that a stale UUID lands in the ledger as `wave: None` plus the failure text — never re-attributed to the worktree or to a registered wave that happens to be nearby.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 187,
      "deletions": 8,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 992,
      "title": "pm reteam: enforce singular team ownership and fence live writers before apply",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf pm reteam --wave designer --dry-run   # see planned moves + deferrals\nlf pm reteam --wave designer --apply     # moves hierarchy only when no Task body is writing\n```\n\n`lf pm reteam --apply` now refuses to mutate when any Task body can still write the old identifier. Fix the named bodies, rerun the dry-run, then apply.\n\n## Summary\n\n`reteam` now treats a Project as needing repair unless it owns *exactly* the wave's bound team — not merely includes it. A Project simultaneously attached to the bound team and a legacy/shared team now counts as a mismatch and gets moved, since one Project belongs to one Wave-owned team. On `--apply`, a new safety gate (`ensure_reteam_apply_safe`) stops before any mutation when deferrals (live Task bodies) are present, preserving the hierarchy rather than moving its Project and idle siblings around a protected Task.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Renamed `project_off_team` → `project_needs_reteam`; requires exactly one team matching the bound team (multi-team and empty sets now flag)\n- Added `ensure_reteam_apply_safe`: `--apply` errors out listing each deferred Task body instead of partial-moving the hierarchy\n- `pm sync` diagnostics use the stricter mismatch so stranded *and* dual-attached Projects surface the `reteam` repair\n- Updated `docs/ops.md` and `docs/wave-authoring.md` help lines to match the new apply semantics\n- Tests cover singular-ownership matching and the apply-stops-before-mutation gate",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 74,
      "deletions": 22,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 994,
      "title": "task: route existing Task Sessions to the successor Project Session",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task status <TASK-ID>\n# project: ps_abandoned → routes to ps_live\n\n# broken chain:\n# project: ps_abandoned → no live successor; resume or restart the Project\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA Task Session records the Project Session it was born under. When that Project Session goes terminal (abandoned or completed) and a new session takes over the same Linear project, the Task was stranded: its observations queued to a dead recipient, its status-triggered reconciliation never ran, and reviews assigned to the predecessor could not be conducted by the live successor. This PR separates provenance from routing. `task.project_session_id` stays as the historical owner and is never rewritten; routing resolves through `resolve_task_project_route`, which returns the historical session when it is still live and its non-terminal successor for the same Linear project when it is not. A terminal historical session with no live successor fails actionably, telling the operator to resume or restart the Project rather than silently dropping work.\n\nThe successor now consumes the whole project chain: `pending_project_observations_for_chain` reads observations addressed to any session for the same Linear project, so a live successor drains what a predecessor was addressed. Pending-observation counts follow the same split — a terminal session counts only its own outbox (draining to zero as the successor consumes), a live session counts the chain. Reconciliation in the Project runner filters Tasks by Linear project id instead of exact session id, so Tasks born under a dead predecessor are supervised by the live session.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `resolve_task_project_route` in `ops/project.rs` returns historical, current, and whether routing followed a successor; observation wakes target the live successor and degrade to a warning (never a lost observation) when the chain is broken\n- Review message/complete authority checks accept the live successor for the same Linear project; the acting session — not the historical assignee — is recorded as conductor\n- `pending_project_observations_for_chain` added to the store and SQLite backend; chain-aware pending counts in project and wave snapshots\n- `TaskSessionSnapshot` and `TaskRuntimeSnapshot` gain `routing_project_session_id` (plus `project_route_succeeded` on the task snapshot); a broken chain reads as `None` rather than a failed status read\n- Swift `WaveWorkMap` and the `active_sessions_census` DTO fixture mirror the new field",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 742,
      "deletions": 48,
      "changed_files": 12
    },
    {
      "number": 995,
      "title": "task: refuse unsafe worktree state before committing a recovered Task successor",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# explicit resume of a Task whose body vanished\nlf task resume W2-251\n\n# supervised recovery — the project observation loop takes the same gate\nlf project observe\n```\n\nA Task on an unrelated branch, a dirty between-PR worktree, or a half-finished rebase now refuses recovery up front:\n\n```\nTask W2-251 between-PR recovery expected settled branch \"jack/task-pr-proof\" or\nnext branch \"jack/task-pr-proof-2\", but the worktree is on \"jack/unrelated\";\nrecovery refused before moving any ownership\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nTask recovery — both the explicit resume and the supervisor's observation loop — used to reap a dead lease, move status, and launch a successor body before PR rotation ever looked at the worktree. A recovery with no active PR would commit the successor and only then have `ensure_working_pr` reject an unrelated branch, leaving the predecessor, the successor link, and the PR sequence in a state nobody asked for.\n\nThis PR computes every branch/worktree/PR adoption precondition read-only, before any durable ownership moves. `task_recovery_adoption` checks that the worktree exists, isn't mid-rebase/merge/cherry-pick/revert/bisect, is on a live branch, and that the branch matches either the active PR or — between PRs — the settled branch or the deterministic next serial branch. A refusal touches neither the store, the lease, the PR sequence, nor the worktree, so the Task is exactly where the operator left it.\n\nThe next-branch computation is factored out of the rotation (`next_pr_slug` / `deterministic_next_branch`) so the gate and the rotation agree — a partially rotated worktree already sitting on the next branch is adopted, not refused.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `engine::git::intervention_state` reports a worktree's crash boundary (rebase, merge, cherry-pick, revert, bisect), reading the worktree's own git dir so linked worktrees inspect their private state\n- `task_recovery_adoption` gates `resume_task_async` and the supervisor's `inspect_outcome`; in the supervisor, one bad Task is skipped with a warning instead of aborting the whole project observation\n- `refuse_dirty_between_prs` runs after PR reconciliation, since reconcile can settle an out-of-band merge and drop the Task into a between-PR state mid-flight\n- Supervised stall recovery clears the gate on a restart plan; an uncertain plan launches nothing, so it still records the loss honestly and leaves the refusal to the explicit resume that follows\n- Tests cover unrelated branch, dirty worktree, missing branch, active PR mismatch, supervised restart, and each crash boundary; the unrelated-branch test also proves predecessor, successor link, PR sequence, lease, and worktree are untouched after refusal",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 688,
      "deletions": 30,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 996,
      "title": "supervision: recover replay-safe stalled task bodies",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# A live Project Session runner supervises its Task bodies automatically.\n# Trigger: a Task body with an Active lease goes 30 min without durable progress.\nlf project run <wave>/<project>\n```\n\nRecovery runs on the Project runner's existing control tick — no new watchdog process. When a Task body stalls, supervision revokes its lease, reaps the tmux process group, and either restarts the same Task Session at generation+1 or parks it for human input.\n\n## Summary\n\nTask bodies could stall silently (live tmux pane, no durable progress) with no owner to recover them. This adds parent-driven supervision: a live Project runner observes each Task body's progress lease and recovers stalled ones. The critical safety property is replay-safety — if any command was mid-delivery when the body stalled, supervision refuses to replay it (which could double-execute a steer or follow-up). Instead it marks those commands `Uncertain`, records the loss, and waits for a human to inspect the transcript and resume explicitly. Only when delivery state is unambiguous does it restart cleanly.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `body_progress_age` and `BodyRecoveryPlan` (`LeaveAlone` / `Restart` / `NeedsInput`) move into `child_session` so both the read model and supervision share one definition of progress and recovery\n- `plan_body_recovery` classifies a stalled body: ambiguous deliveries (Delivering/Uncertain, same generation) become `NeedsInput`; otherwise `Restart` — uncertainty from older generations never poisons a successor\n- `reap_revoked_child_body` split out of `revoke_and_reap_child_body` so supervision can reap an already-revoked generation\n- `supervise_project_task_bodies` walks a Project's live Task bodies, observes each, and recovers stalled ones via `recover_stalled_task_body`, which records `Superseded` (restart) or `Lost` (needs input) outcomes\n- `mark_stale_child_deliveries_uncertain` + `revoke_task_process_if_unchanged` make the recovery compare-and-set: a body that advanced between observation and revocation is left alone\n- `TaskSession::supervisor_restart_bar` gates recovery when a Task has an active PR or other reason not to restart",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 705,
      "deletions": 30,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 997,
      "title": "ops: resolve Actions run/job ids in lf wt ci --logs",
      "body": "## Summary\n\n`lf wt ci --logs` passed full GitHub Actions job URLs as the run identifier to `gh run view`, producing malformed API paths like `/actions/runs/https://github.com/...` and HTTP 404 (seen on PRs #978 and #983).\n\n- Parse the check `detailsUrl` (job URL, run URL) or a bare numeric run id into a run/job id before calling `gh run view --log [--job ...]`.\n- Print each failed check's logs under a `### <name>` attribution header.\n- Report missing/expired/private logs and non-Actions (external CI) URLs actionably with the run id and a link, instead of silently dropping them.\n- Integration test reproduces the PR 978 (job URL) and PR 983 (run URL) shapes plus the numeric-id variant, and proves no full URL ever reaches `gh run view`.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 469,
      "deletions": 22,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 998,
      "title": "handoff: attach an IDE for Claude sessions with a known session id",
      "body": "## Try it\n\nOpen the handoff sheet for a Claude session on a local Home, pick VS Code or Cursor:\n\n```\nlf task <id>   # then: Attach → VS Code / Cursor\n```\n\nThe IDE opens at the worktree and runs the exact shared attach command in its integrated terminal:\n\n```bash\ncd '/src/repo' && exec 'claude' '--resume' 'sess_abc123' '--cwd' '/src/repo'\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAn IDE used to be capped at `.worktreeOnly` — it opened the folder but never resumed the durable provider Session, so the sheet always labeled it as the weaker action. When the provider is Claude and a specific provider session id is known, the IDE can now claim `.attach`: `HandoffSurfaceLauncher` opens the workspace, then drives the command palette via AppleScript to create an integrated terminal and run the same argv Ghostty and Warp run. Every other case (non-Claude provider, unknown session id, remote Home) resolves exactly as before.\n\nThe honesty invariant survives the new path. `launch` now returns a three-way `HandoffLaunchResult` instead of a bool, because opening the IDE and failing to run the command is a real third outcome — if the AppleScript can't run (Accessibility denied, say), the IDE is already open at the worktree, so the result is `.worktreeOnly`: the sheet says so plainly, and the launch never records itself as the last-attach preference. Only `.failed` falls back to the embedded Ghostty terminal.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `HandoffSurfaceCapability` gains `providerIsClaude` and `providerSessionKnown`; `reach(.vscode/.cursor)` promotes to `.attach` only when both hold on a proven local workspace\n- `HandoffSurfaceLauncher.capability` takes the provider and session id; `launch` returns `HandoffLaunchResult` (`.attached` / `.worktreeOnly` / `.failed`)\n- New `ideShellCommand` and `ideAttachAppleScript` — pure builders, so the exact command and the script that types it are both directly testable\n- `ActiveSessionsView` switches on the three outcomes and only records a preference on `.attached`\n- CI: clear `DerivedData/ModuleCache.noindex` before the Loopflow build. A runner SDK update bumps the shims' `module.modulemap` mtime without touching `Package.resolved` or `project.yml`, so an exact cache-key hit restores `.pcm` files built against the old SDK and fails with \"file has been modified since the module file was built\". Only precompiled modules are dropped; the expensive object-file cache next to it stays valid.\n- Drops a tracked scratch doc",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 350,
      "deletions": 68,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 999,
      "title": "ops: extract telemetry into a module with path-contract guard",
      "body": "",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 181,
      "deletions": 99,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1000,
      "title": "harness: reap orphaned opencode process groups on resident boot",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# Crash a resident so its opencode tree is orphaned, then boot again:\nlf wave infrastructure   # startup sweep clears the leftover server + descendants\nlf top                   # shows the lf body and the opencode serve leader, not its children\n\ncargo test -p loopflow harness::opencode\ncargo test -p loopflow harness::opencode_runtime\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`opencode serve` spawns a tree — MCP servers, model proxies, npm-shim grandchildren — and every path that shut it down only killed the direct child, so the descendants survived as orphans holding ports and model connections. This makes the harness spawn the server in its own process group (`process_group(0)`, so the pid *is* the group id) and kills the whole group everywhere it matters: `stop()`, harness drop, and a new interrupt-cleanup hook that fires on SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP (the signal handler exits before destructors run, so `kill_on_drop` never fires there — that's the `tmux kill-session` hole).\n\nThe startup reaper now works in group terms too. It classifies each registered leader as Dead, Opencode, or Other: a live opencode leader gets its group reaped, a dead leader with a live group still gets reaped (the orphaned-leader shape), and a pid recycled into an unrelated process is left alone and pruned from the registry. Reaping is SIGTERM → 2s grace → SIGKILL, so the server gets a chance to close its port cleanly, with a guard refusing to signal our own process group. `lf __resident` runs this sweep before registering its own server, so a resident that follows a crashed one starts clean.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `opencode.rs`: own process group, group kill in `stop()`, `kill_on_drop`, interrupt-cleanup hook, `process_group_id()` on the harness\n- `opencode_runtime.rs`: `LeaderState` classification, group liveness probe, TERM/grace/KILL group termination, registry pruning for recycled pids; `pid_is_alive` uses `libc::kill` instead of shelling out to `kill -0`\n- `resident.rs`: startup orphan sweep before the resident registers its server\n- Tests cover both real shapes end-to-end with real processes: an orphaned leader whose grandchild must not outlive the group kill, and a live parent leader plus provider child that must both come down. `lf top` gains a test proving it keeps the `opencode serve` leader (the unit the reaper reaps) and filters the descendants, so the view never double-counts the tree.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 505,
      "deletions": 45,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1001,
      "title": "task: surface CI-fix infrastructure failures as blocked",
      "body": "Classifies infrastructure failures during ci-fix as an actionable blocked Task state instead of generic waiting. Durable PR context and failing-check evidence remain attached, and focused tests prove no silent loss or false progress.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 349,
      "deletions": 25,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1002,
      "title": "lf status: treat terminal Sessions for an absent Project as history, not a fatal error",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# A Wave whose Sessions reference a Project the local PM snapshot omits\nlf status product --json\n\n# Recover a genuinely-gone Project referenced by a live Session\nlf pm sync --wave product\nlf project abandon product-performance --reason \"Project is absent from the current PM snapshot\"\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`snapshot_projects` used to hard-error whenever any Session referenced a Project missing from the current PM snapshot. That included Sessions that had already settled — an abandoned Project Session or a completed Task Session from a Project that was later archived would poison every subsequent `lf status <wave>` read, and the Mac app got a non-zero exit with no JSON at all.\n\nSession-to-snapshot resolution now splits on whether the Session is still live. Terminal Sessions (abandoned, completed) for an absent Project are history: they're skipped, and status renders the rest of the Wave. Non-terminal Sessions still fail loudly, because a live Session pointing at a Project nobody can see is a real inconsistency — but the error now names the specific Session, the Wave to sync, and the exact command to settle the stale Session if the Project is truly gone.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `session_project_index` replaces the bare `project_index` lookup on the Session paths; `find_project_index` holds the shared positional lookup so the pure-snapshot path (`project_index`) keeps its existing error.\n- Missing-Project errors carry recovery commands: `lf pm sync --wave <wave>` plus `lf project abandon <slug>` / `lf task abandon <identifier>`.\n- Tests cover the three behaviors: terminal Sessions for an absent Project render an empty (not failed) status; non-terminal Sessions error with the Session id and both recovery commands; and a refreshed PM snapshot rebuilds the Project/KR/Task hierarchy and reattaches the running Session without a restart.\n- `scratch/self-heal-pm-snapshots-when.md` records the design, including the two follow-on pieces not in this PR — wrapping `WaveDetailSnapshot.projects` in `Evidence` so a missing Project can't take the whole status read down, and a bounded auto-refresh before erroring.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 369,
      "deletions": 7,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 1003,
      "title": "ops: resolve ambient Wave names and UUIDs uniformly in Task and Project controls",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Both accept a Wave name or UUID in LF_WAVE_ID\nLF_WAVE_ID=infrastructure lf task pause INF-123\nLF_WAVE_ID=$(lf wave id infrastructure) lf project resume my-project\n\n# A stale or foreign ambient Wave now fails loudly instead of passing as Human\nLF_WAVE_ID=ghost-wave lf task pause INF-123   # error: not registered\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nTask and Project controls each had their own copy of ambient-Wave classification, and both only understood a raw UUID in `LF_WAVE_ID`. A Wave *name* — the form the rest of the CLI accepts — either failed to parse or, worse, could slip through as a `Human` command, silently laundering a foreign or stale identity into an authorized one. Both controls now call a single `resolve_child_command_source` helper in `ops::util`, which goes through `resolve_managed_wave_name` and the store registry, so names and UUIDs resolve identically in both places and cannot drift apart again.\n\nClassification is now explicit: an ambient Wave matching the owner becomes `Wave`; a different registered Wave is a \"cannot control\" error naming both Waves; an unregistered name or UUID is a \"stale context\" error; only a genuinely absent context yields `Human`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `ops::util::resolve_child_command_source` — the one place Task and Project controls classify ambient Wave identity; async, because it reads the wave registry.\n- `command_source` (task) and `project_command_source` both delegate to it; the duplicated env-parsing and the `command_source_for_wave` helper are gone, along with its unit test.\n- Error messages use Wave names rather than UUIDs, falling back to the UUID if the owning Wave isn't registered.\n- Test coverage moves to a single classification-matrix test over real store rows in `ops/util.rs`, covering owning-by-name, owning-by-UUID, stale UUID, stale name, absent, and foreign-by-name/UUID.\n- Integration `EnvGuard` now also clears `LF_WAVE_ID` and `LF_PROJECT_SESSION_ID`, so the suite doesn't inherit the ambient Wave when it runs inside a live Task Session.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 220,
      "deletions": 75,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1004,
      "title": "task: fail PR operations closed without durable registry authority (W2-253)",
      "body": "",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 667,
      "deletions": 30,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1005,
      "title": "wave: prove the complete CLI wave-resolution matrix",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow --test wave_resolution_matrix\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nEvery Wave-scoped `lf` command now resolves its Wave the same way, and a table-driven integration test proves it. `rust/loopflow/tests/wave_resolution_matrix.rs` runs each read and mutation across seven ambient environments (no context, explicit `--wave`, durable `LF_WAVE_ID`, stale UUID, hand-set name, and so on) and asserts every command in the same environment classifies identically. A completeness guard walks the clap tree and fails CI when a new `--wave`-bearing command shows up unregistered, so the matrix can't silently fall behind the CLI.\n\nThe five commands W2-151 left diverging — `lf home probe`, `lf reviews catch-up`, `lf roadmap`, `lf project start`, `lf project promote` — are aligned onto the shared validating resolver. They previously invented their own rules: some dropped a stale `LF_WAVE_ID` silently into global scope, some refused ambient context entirely. Now `--wave` wins, else `LF_WAVE_ID` (durable UUID → registry name, hand-set name as fallback), and a stale UUID is a loud error. `lf roadmap` and `lf pm status` keep `NoContext` as a legitimate global default, which the matrix encodes explicitly rather than leaving to chance.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `wave_resolution_matrix` integration test: seven ambient environments × every Wave-scoped command, plus a clap-tree completeness guard\n- `home probe`, `reviews catch-up`, `roadmap`, `project start`, `project promote` route through `resolve_managed_wave_name{,_sync}` and map `NoContext` to their own message\n- `ops::resolve_wave_name` deleted in favor of `ops::normalize_wave_name` — the old name implied resolution but only normalized, which is how callers ended up skipping ambient context\n- Callers that genuinely want normalize-only (`wave run`/`stop`/`resident`, `pm --wave`) now say so at the call site",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1124,
      "deletions": 32,
      "changed_files": 12
    },
    {
      "number": 1006,
      "title": "task: require an authoritative non-empty range for every task pr operation",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# In a Task worktree whose branch has no commits past the recorded base:\nlf pr open create\nlf pr land\n# => Task INF-123 PR range is empty: HEAD is the recorded base abc123456789,\n#    so the PR has no commits to publish. Commit the Task's work, or complete\n#    the Task directly if the work is done. Refused before any GitHub side effect.\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nEvery Task PR operation now proves its range is both authoritative and non-empty before touching GitHub. The old `task_pr_has_changes` guard lived in `request_task_pr_publication` and only fired when `pr.github().is_none()` — so an already-open PR that got reset or rebased empty sailed straight through to `gh pr edit/ready/merge`. It also compared against `origin/<default>` rather than the recorded base, so it disagreed with `lf task changes` whenever the upstream had advanced.\n\nThe replacement is `require_task_pr_range_nonempty`, called from both `pr::create_or_update_pr` and `land::prepare_pr` at the point where the publication path has already committed pending work and just before the first `gh pr` call. It runs the existing ancestry parity proof (which heals a stale recorded base in place), re-reads the PR, then refuses when HEAD is the recorded base or the tree at HEAD matches it. The emptiness check uses the recorded `base_commit`, not a recomputed merge-base, so the refusal describes the same range the user sees in `lf task changes`. It is unconditional — an open PR gets the same gate as a first publication.\n\nStacked children needed a fix to make this hold: measuring a child's ancestry against `origin/<default>` counts the parent's commits as foreign contamination. `resolve_verifier_upstream` now measures a child with a live parent against the parent's branch tip, and falls back to `origin/<default>` once the parent merges or is abandoned (at which point `record_stack_rebase` has already collapsed the child onto the default branch).\n\n## Changes\n\n- `verify_task_pr_range` stays the ancestry-only gate that runs before push; `require_task_pr_range_nonempty` is the stronger gate for anything with a GitHub side effect\n- Deleted `task_pr_has_changes` and its conditional call site in `request_task_pr_publication`\n- New `resolve_verifier_upstream` picks the parent branch tip as the upstream for a live stacked child\n- Tests cover HEAD-at-base, a zero-net-tree-change range, the previously-skipped already-open-PR case, and stacked-child ancestry against a live parent\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 599,
      "deletions": 48,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1007,
      "title": "store: preserve external Task direction across recovery",
      "body": "Carries the Linear observation cursor, title/body revision, and ingested-comment ledger from a terminal Task predecessor to its successor transactionally. Recovery is idempotent, historical receipts remain attributable, and deterministic store/webhook tests prove edits and comments racing succession are delivered exactly once.",
