    },
    {
      "number": 1010,
      "title": "task: link published PRs to their Linear issue",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf pr open create\nlf task status\n```\n\nOpening, submitting, or landing a task PR now writes the linkage back to the task's Linear issue: a first-class attachment pointing at the GitHub PR, plus a loopflow-managed comment carrying the publication state. Re-running any publication command refreshes the same attachment and comment in place rather than piling up duplicates — the stored `linear_attachment_id` / `linear_comment_id` switch the writeback from create to update.\n\nThe writeback is best-effort and never fails the publication: the GitHub PR has already been opened by the time Linear is touched, so a failure is recorded on the PR as `linear_link_error` and retried by the next publication command. Partial progress survives — if the attachment lands but the comment write fails, the attachment id rides forward so the retry updates it in place and only creates what's missing. A degraded linkage is named in `lf task status` on the PR line, directly beneath the existing `PM writeback` reading, so an expired token can't fail silently forever. Silence means linked.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New Linear client methods for `attachmentLinkURL` / `attachmentUpdate` / `commentUpdate`; `comment` now returns the created comment id\n- `pm_link_pr_async` in `ops/pm` performs the idempotent upsert and returns the ids obtained so far alongside any failure message\n- Three new `TaskPr` fields (`linear_attachment_id`, `linear_comment_id`, `linear_link_error`) with migration `0.11.020_task_pr_linear_linkage.sql`\n- The attachment subtitle and comment body are derived from the PR's phase and after-merge disposition, so the label is a projection of the PR model rather than a second state to keep in sync\n- `lf task status` PR line extracted into `format_task_pr_line` and covered by tests for the healthy and degraded readings",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 774,
      "deletions": 29,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 1011,
      "title": "task actions: derive a truthful legal-action model from total evidence",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task status\n# ...\n#   action: review  (checks passed; awaiting review)\n#     blocked: resume  (PR is open and awaiting review)\n\ncargo test -p loopflow task::actions\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nTask lifecycle affordances were re-derived independently on every surface: `lf status` computed a `TaskAttentionControl` list from runtime status plus process liveness, the Mac app inferred its own buttons from the work map, and neither knew about PR phase, CI, the review gate, or abandon intent. That split-brain let surfaces disagree — a Task waiting on a passing open PR would still advertise Resume.\n\nThis replaces that with a single pure function. `derive_task_actions` in the new `rust/loopflow/src/task/actions.rs` takes one total evidence bundle (`TaskActionEvidence`: session status, active PR phase, after-merge/next-slug publication intent, fresh CI, process liveness, predecessor PR phase, review gate, abandon intent, unsettled local progress) and returns a `TaskActionModel` — all six actions (`Recover`, `Resume`, `Review`, `StartNextPr`, `Complete`, `NoAction`) each carrying `available` plus the reason it's legal or the fact blocking it, and a single `recommended` pick. Every surface consumes the model; none re-derives it.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `task::actions` module owning the action enum, evidence bundle, review-gate state, and the derivation, with the reason-matrix pinned by tests. `ci_failure_reason` moves here from `lf/commands/waves.rs`.\n- `TaskAttentionSnapshot.controls` → `actions: TaskActionModel`; `snapshot_task_detail` and `task_snapshot` now read the parent PR phase and the phase-scoped interaction review to fill the evidence bundle.\n- `lf task status` prints the recommended action with its reason and lists blocked actions with the blocking fact.\n- `BodyControl::Attach` added so a stalled or steerable body advertises attaching alongside steer/interrupt/stop.\n- Swift `WaveWorkMap`, `RoadmapView`, and `TaskWorkspaceView` mirror the action model instead of inferring buttons locally; tests follow.\n- DTO fixtures (`active_sessions_census`, `roadmap_snapshot`, `task_attention_states`, `wave_detail`) migrated to the action shape and expanded to cover the matrix.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3297,
      "deletions": 253,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 1012,
      "title": "lfd: durable delivery inbox for Home subscription ingress",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo build --bin lfd\n\n# run it on a Home machine (store must already exist)\ndoppler run -- lfd serve --addr 127.0.0.1:8080\n\n# or keep it up across reboots (launchd on macOS, systemd user on Linux)\nlfd install --addr 127.0.0.1:8080\nlfd status\nlfd uninstall\n\ncurl localhost:8080/health   # {\"status\":\"ok\"}\ncurl localhost:8080/status   # {\"waves\":N,\"deliveries\":N}\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds `lfd`, the machine-level Home daemon: the one process that has to stay up on a Home machine because it receives external HTTP that can't arrive over SSH or `lf`. It is deliberately not an API — reads stay `lf` queries, hands stay `lf`. It serves exactly three routes: `/health` for liveness, `/status` for a wave + delivery count, and `/linear/webhook` for signed provider deliveries.\n\nThe ingress path is a durable inbox. Every verified delivery is persisted to a new `provider_deliveries` table *before* it's acknowledged, deduplicated on `(delivery_id, provider)`, then routed through the existing `webhook::ingest_event` domain ops and stamped with its terminal status. Two gates, on purpose: the inbox dedupes *deliveries* (a Linear redelivery is dropped), the domain tables dedupe *events* (a crash mid-flight leaves the row `pending`, so the retry re-processes and no-ops at the domain instead of being silently swallowed). If the stamp itself fails, the row stays `pending` and the handler 500s so Linear retries.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `lfd` binary with `serve` / `install` / `status` / `uninstall`. `install` renders a launchd or systemd user service; service files never carry secrets — Linear credentials come from Doppler.\n- New `provider_deliveries` store table (migration `0.11.020`) plus the sqlite backend for `record_delivery`, `complete_delivery`, and `delivery_count`.\n- Delivery ids are derived from the parsed event: issue edits key on `issue_id` + the monotonic `updatedAt` revision (matching the domain guard), comments on the globally unique comment id, ignored events on timestamp + body digest.\n- Store is always open — a machine with no registry can't host the daemon. Linear config is optional; without both `LF_LINEAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET` and `LF_LINEAR_VIEWER_ID` the daemon runs and `/linear/webhook` returns 503.\n- Non-loopback binds are refused unless `LF_LFD_AUTH_TOKEN` is set, and warn when they are. Webhook bodies are capped at 256 KiB.\n- Integration tests in `tests/lfd_tests.rs` cover signature rejection, replay window, dedup across redelivery, and pending re-processing.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1605,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 12
    },
    {
      "number": 1013,
