      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 1018,
      "title": "task ci-fix: settle one bounded ci-fix turn at its exit",
      "body": "Settles one bounded ci-fix turn at its exit, and parks.\n\n`settle_ci_fix_turn` is the exit half of `arm_ci_fix_wake` (#1024/#1026): the only\nplace a `CiFix` command goes terminal by running. It settles the exact wake the\nbody was born for — `CiFixWake.command_id` — rather than re-deriving the identity\nfrom a PR that may since name a different failure. The verdict rides the existing\n`decide_open_pr_status`, which already reads an open PR the way the lifecycle\ndoes: `Waiting` accepts, `Blocked` fails with the same operator-facing reason, a\nsettled or vanished PR supersedes, and an interrupted turn settles nothing.\n\nThe body then parks — no gate, no successor step, no PR rotation. A repair pushes\nto an existing branch; advancing the Task on its behalf would credit a repair as\nprogress the Task never made.\n\n**The ordering is the crash contract.** The park and the settlement are two writes\nwith no transaction around them, so the Session is parked *before* the wake goes\nterminal. A death in that window leaves the wake `Claimed` — what\n`relaunch_on_duplicate` documents as recovery's job. The reverse order would spend\nthe incident identity while the Session still read `Running`; `ensure_` mints no\nsecond wake for a spent identity, so the successor would find nothing, silently\nresume the generic lifecycle, and leave the PR red with nobody repairing it.\n\nAlso fixes an outcome lie: `finish_parked` recorded `Interrupted` for every parked\nbody, filing a repair that ran to the end as abandoned work. The outcome is now an\nexplicit parameter the caller supplies.\n\nNo schema, no migration, no lifecycle phase, no new command API — the wake ledger\nand `child_commands` already carry this. Three files.\n\nProofs (extending the #1026 suite): a repaired head accepts its wake and parks\nwithout phase/gate/rotation and reports `Completed`; an unrepaired head fails with\nan actionable reason and the same identity never re-arms; an interrupted turn stays\n`Claimed` and reports `Interrupted`; and the park event durably precedes the\nterminal command event. Each was verified by sabotaging the code it names — the\nordering proof and the outcome proof both fail against the reversed/old behavior.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 400,
      "deletions": 17,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1020,
      "title": "opencode: make SSE hollow-body disconnects observable and recoverable",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nA truncated upstream GLM stream can surface to loopflow as `session.status: idle` with zero content — the old mapping trusted the status and minted a hollow `Completed` turn, advancing the flow step as if the skill ran. This fixes the trust contract: a dispatched loop must land its PR **or** stop with an actionable record — never silently stall.\n\n### Layer 1 — Observability (content-based, not status-based)\n\nThe core insight: detect hollowness by **content**, not by `session.status`. A turn is substantive only if it emitted text, reasoning, a tool call, or a diff. `idle` with no substance → `Failed` + `opencode_hollow_body` (never `Completed`). This is correct whether opencode reports a truncated upstream stream as `idle` or `error` — it doesn't depend on which status opencode chose.\n\n- `opencode_hollow_body`: turn closed `idle` with zero mapped content\n- `opencode_decode_gap`: turn closed `idle` with `output_tokens > 0` but zero mapped content (harness decode failure, not an empty model turn)\n- `opencode_disconnected`: harness `/event` stream dropped; closes the orphaned turn `Failed` **before** the `Error` so every `TurnStarted` gets a terminal close\n\n### Layer 2 — Recovery (backup handoff default, retry exception)\n\nA disconnect-class failure routes to the configured `backup_agent` (GOAL.md frontmatter) via the existing `handoff_{task,project}_body` machinery — only the automatic trigger and one config knob are new. Same-agent retry is the exception, gated on replay-safety (no completed Command/File item this turn). No backup and not replay-safe → stop with a non-convergence record, never silently re-run a side-effecting body.\n\n### Layer 3 — Fencing\n\nThe handoff finishes the dead process before switching the agent. A late-arriving write from the dead opencode body hits a `LeaseRevoked` error — the process state is `Finished`, not `Active`. The backup body owns the next generation.\n\n## Test coverage\n\n- 14 mapping tests: hollow idle close, decode-gap, orphaned turn closure, second-turn independence, tool-only substantive, session error\n- 4-case fake-SSE matrix: pre-content, after-active, mid-tool, after-durable — each asserts no false `Completed`, every `TurnStarted` closed, `opencode_disconnected` present\n- 7 recovery classifier tests: backup routing, replay-safe retry, stop on not-replay-safe, ping-pong prevention, normal non-disconnect passthrough\n- 1 end-to-end fencing test: disconnect failure with backup → `BodyHandedOff` event, failed generation retained, late write rejected\n\nAll 1490 lib tests pass. `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` clean.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Operator 10-body GLM validation run (operator step, tracked in Done-when)\n- Claude/Codex harness behavior (Claude fails the turn on crash by construction; Codex disconnect is its own provider surface)\n- No GLM-name special case — the fix is in the shared opencode harness and child-supervision contract",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1260,
      "deletions": 68,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 1021,
      "title": "ci: measure failed PR recovery across the Home",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ci --since 7d                              # every CI repair attempt on this Home\nlf ci --since 24h --wave infrastructure       # scope to one Wave\nlf ci --since 7d --repo owner/repo --json     # complete machine-wide incident receipt\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nFailed-CI recovery had no evidence trail: we could see that a Task eventually merged, but not whether the loop detected the failure, how long it took to respond, or whether a human had to step in. This adds a durable CI incident ledger to the Home store and a `lf ci` reader over it.\n\nOne failed head is one repair attempt. The Task runner records the incident when CI goes red — failed head sha, failure set, provider completion time, and whether detection came from webhook or poll — then stamps the response, green, merge, and blocked transitions as they happen. Later passing and merge observations close every open attempt on that PR, so a PR that failed three times shows three attempts, each with its own latency.\n\n`lf ci` reads that ledger locally without touching GitHub. It prints per-incident detect/respond/green/merge latency and outcome, plus a summary with median and p95 for each stage and an autonomous vs. human-assisted split — an incident counts as human-assisted when Human or Linear direction entered the loop.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `ci_incidents` table (migration `0.11.024`) with store reads/writes and report rows joining Wave, Task, status, and direction provenance\n- Task runner records incidents on failure and resolves open attempts on green/merge/blocked\n- New `lf ci` command with `--since` (relative `7d`/`24h`/`30m` or RFC3339), `--wave`, `--repo`, and `--json`\n- README and `docs/lf.md` cover the command and its local-only, one-failed-head-per-attempt semantics\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1150,
      "deletions": 23,
      "changed_files": 19
    },
    {
      "number": 1022,
      "title": "usage: one spend grain, subscription-first lf usage",
      "body": "## What\n\nUnifies loopflow's usage tracking around one rule and makes `lf usage` answer the question that matters on a subscription: **how much of each account's plan is left**.\n\n### One spend grain\n- `run_events` usage rows are now **per-boundary deltas** written at drain time; readers sum, never diff. `own_spend`/`boundary_spans` diffing and the doctor's cross-store reconciliation are deleted. `lf trace` sums its process rows. The `--json` wire shape (SpanDto) is unchanged — the Mac dashboard keeps reconciling with `lf usage` without edits.\n- Codex turn receipts were **thread-cumulative snapshots** (summing the live DB gave 9.6B \"input\" tokens; `lf runs` was lying for codex). The harness now emits true per-turn deltas — resumed threads baseline out prior-launch history via the request-sized `last` report — with input **net of cache reads**, the shape Claude already reports. The exec stream now reads `cached_input_tokens` at all (it was dropped, which is why `lf usage` showed codex cache = 0 against a 93%-cached reality).\n- Migration `0.11.025` converts historical rows to deltas (verified against a copy of the production ledger: claude totals preserved exactly), delta-izes codex turns by provider session, and purges the 104 `.tmp*` test-residue repos that predate #908's store isolation.\n\n### Subscription-first `lf usage`\n```\nPROVIDER      ACCOUNT                         PLAN    SESSION         WEEKLY          NOTE\nclaude        jack@loopflow.studio            max     22% → 16:20     29% → Jul 20\nclaude        jackstah@gmail.com, loopflow-…  max     -               -               needs re-login: lf auth connect claude\ncodex         loopflow-eng@loopflow.studio    max     -               -               needs re-login: lf auth connect codex\n```\n- New `provider_account_limits` table, fed two ways: harness rate-limit signals now persist **full window snapshots** mid-run (free), and a new `subscription` module polls on demand — Claude via the OAuth usage endpoint (refreshing the stored token and writing the rotated pair back), Codex via a one-shot `codex app-server` `account/rateLimits/read`. Stale observations (>15 min) re-poll automatically; `--refresh` forces, `--cached` skips.\n- Revoked credentials surface as the fix (`lf auth connect <provider>`), never a blank row.\n- The spend table drops the COST column — codex never reported cost and claude's number was API-equivalent dollars nobody pays on a subscription — for a SHARE-of-total-tokens column. Cost stays in `--json` for machines.\n\n## Verified\n- Full migration dry-run against a `.backup` copy of the live production ledger: claude totals byte-identical to the old `own_spend` output, junk gone, FK check clean.\n- Live end-to-end run against that copy: real Claude poll (token refresh + usage), real codex app-server poll, output above.\n- 1482 unit tests + full integration suite green; `cargo fmt`, `clippy --all-targets -D warnings` clean.\n\n## Known follow-ups\n- Task/project session runners still ignore `TurnUsage`, so harness-driven session spend reaches `agent_turns` but not the run ledger (pre-existing coverage gap, now documented).\n- Historical codex ledger rows keep gross input (the cache split was never recorded and is unrecoverable); only forward rows are net-of-cache.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1335,
      "deletions": 260,
      "changed_files": 21
    },
    {
      "number": 1023,
      "title": "auth: adopt stored codex logins and stop opening the loopback listener",
      "body": "## What\n\nTwo `lf auth connect`/`import` UX bugs observed reconnecting accounts today:\n\n1. **Dead localhost tab on codex connect.** `codex login` announces its local callback server (`http://localhost:1455`) before printing the real authorization URL. The generic auth-line parser took the *first* URL as the verification URL, so `lf` opened a \"Not Found\" tab at the listener root and completed the flow before ever reading the real URL (the correct page only appeared because codex opens it itself — macOS ignores `BROWSER=echo`). Loopback URLs are now never verification URLs; the flow waits for the real one, which `lf` then opens in the bound Chrome profile.\n\n2. **`lf auth import codex` refused.** The claude-only guard predated codex support in the underlying machinery (broker status check, token extraction, registration all handle codex). Import now adopts an existing `auth.json` in the account home — so a login that already lives under `~/.lf/accounts/codex/<account>` (e.g. `manabot-eng`, whose stored credential is valid) registers without a fresh browser dance. The ambient-login fallback stays Claude-only and the error says so.\n\n## Verified\n- New parser test replays the codex login line sequence: loopback line alone must not complete the flow; the following real URL wins.\n- New import test: codex import with no stored credential errors with the ambient-limit message (isolated `LF_HOME`).\n- 1495 unit tests green; fmt + clippy clean.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 73,
      "deletions": 12,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 1024,
      "title": "task ci-fix: route automatic ci-fix wakes through the durable command ledger",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Observe a Task's PR; a failed required-check head now enqueues a durable wake\nlf ci incidents --json | jq '.[] | {identity, trigger_command_id, responded_at}'\n\ncargo test -p loopflow ci_fix\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAn automatic `ci-fix` wake used to be a bespoke in-process call (`ops::child::wake_task_ci_fix`) that launched a generation directly. Nothing durable recorded that the wake happened, so a crash between \"body armed\" and \"incident stamped\" left an incident reading *responded, trigger unknown*, and any surface could wake a repair body without the ledger knowing. This PR deletes that side door: a wake is now a `ChildCommandKind::CiFix` command flowing through `queue_command` like every other child command, deduplicated on the incident identity, and linked to its incident before anything can service it.\n\nThe command carries the launch intent rather than input to a live turn — its payload becomes the born body's seed, and it has no `effect`, so it never mints a directive that would block Task completion on a direction no human gave. It stays `Claimed` for the whole bounded repair turn, which makes the reclaim itself the durable \"this body exists to repair CI\" signal across a crash; settling it is the repair turn's job.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `ChildCommandKind::CiFix { incident_identity, pr_number, head_sha, failing_checks }`, riding `child_commands.kind_json` — no migration needed. Round-trip and no-effect tests cover the wire shape.\n- `store::ensure_child_ci_fix_command` dedups on incident identity: one (repo, PR, failed head, failure set) mints one wake, forever.\n- `CiIncident` gains `trigger_command_id`, surfaced on `CiIncidentDto`. The durable order is incident exists → command ensure → trigger link → launch/claim/respond; an unlinkable wake is refused rather than launched, since a repair nothing can attribute is the exact state this path prevents.\n- `relaunch_on_duplicate` keys `CiFix` on `Persisted` instead of not-terminal: a supervision pass re-sends the wake every poll, and the old rule would spin a body for as long as the repair was unsettled. A dead claimed generation is recovery's problem — recovery re-enters ci-fix and reclaims the same command.\n- `absorb_commands` supersedes a `CiFix` that reaches a live body: the observer saw an inactive Session and raced a body that was starting. Superseded, not failed — a moot wake is not a fault, and the reason rides the event rather than the `error` column.\n- `TaskPr` drops `woken_failure_set`; the command ledger, not a field on the PR, now answers \"has this failure already woken a body?\"\n- Removed `wake_task_ci_fix`. `TaskSession::ci_fix_restart_bar` still decides legality; the ledger decides whether the wake already fired.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1502,
      "deletions": 323,
      "changed_files": 16
    },
    {
      "number": 1025,
      "title": "usage: provider-reported plans and unambiguous window headers",
      "body": "## What\n\nThree display truths from first live use of the new `lf usage`:\n\n- **PLAN is observed, not declared.** Codex `planType` rides every rate-limit snapshot; claude's `subscriptionType` lives in the credential file Claude Code maintains. Display now prefers those; the hand-entered `lf auth set --plan` value is only a fallback for accounts never yet observed. (Live symptom: a codex account labeled `max` by hand while the provider says `pro`.)\n- **Used vs remaining is now explicit.** Columns read `SESSION USED` / `WEEKLY USED` — `0% → Jul 23` means a fresh window, not an exhausted one.\n- **`SHARE` → `% TOKENS`.** It is each row's slice of all tokens in the window — a distribution across repos, deliberately not a subscription measure (one repo can burn many subscriptions' worth; subscription state lives in the ACCOUNTS section).\n\n## Verified\n- 1502 unit tests green; fmt + `clippy --all-targets -D warnings` clean.\n- Claude plan captured in the window rows in the parser test.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 44,
      "deletions": 20,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1026,
      "title": "task ci-fix: prove a crashed ci-fix repair resumes on its own wake",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ci_fix_lifecycle\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds the test that justifies holding a ci-fix wake in `Claimed` through the repair turn. A body arms the wake, dies mid-repair, and its successor generation must land back on the *same* command and reselect ci-fix. That only works because the command is still `Claimed` — `claim_child_commands_in` reassigns `persisted`/`claimed` rows to the asking generation and skips terminal ones. Both rejected designs fail this test, which is the point: accepting at arm makes the wake terminal and the PR stays red with nobody repairing it; delivering at arm lets `reconcile_stale_deliveries` flip the dead generation to `Uncertain`, stranding an automatic wake on a human via `NeedsInput`.\n\nA second test pins the wake-is-a-command boundary: minting a `ChildDirective` would bump `current_directive_version`, and `has_pending_directive` would then gate `task_completion_gate` on a direction no human ever gave — blocking Task completion.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `crash_and_relaunch` harness helper performs a real succession (revoke, finish, reserve, activate) rather than re-arming one lease, since re-arming can never prove a *successor* reclaims\n- Crash test asserts the successor reclaims the same command with the same head payload, and that the crash mints no second wake or incident\n- Directive test asserts a wake leaves both directive versions untouched and binds no directive to the wake command\n- Drops a redundant wake-state test now covered by the above",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 191,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 1027,
      "title": "auth: exec a provider CLI on a managed account's home",
      "body": "## What\n\n`lf auth exec <provider> <account> [args…]` — replaces the process with the vendor CLI (codex/claude) running on the managed account's credential home, with ambient token env vars cleared.\n\nWhy: logging into a managed account with a bare `codex login` mints a fresh session and **evicts the managed one** (observed today: loopflow-eng went \"needs re-login\" hours after reconnecting because of a direct login). One account should have one credential home; exec makes direct interactive use share the session lf routes through.\n\n```bash\nlf auth exec codex engineering                       # interactive codex, managed session\nlf auth exec codex manabot-eng@loopflow.studio -- login status   # accepts login email too\n```\n\n## Verified\n- Live: `lf auth exec codex manabot-eng@loopflow.studio -- login status` → \"Logged in using ChatGPT\" against the managed home.\n- 1511 unit tests green; fmt + clippy --all-targets clean.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 72,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1028,
      "title": "journal: never point at a parent the ledger never recorded",
      "body": "",
