    },
    {
      "number": 1049,
      "title": "lfd: bound daemon log storage",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlfd install --repo /path/to/repo\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nKeep daemon logs from becoming another source of disk pressure. `lfd` now owns a bounded 8 MiB current log plus one predecessor, while launchd and systemd stop duplicating output into unbounded service files.\n\n## Changes\n\n- rotate `lfd.log` at 8 MiB and retain one predecessor\n- honor `LF_HOME` for the log location\n- send launchd/systemd stdout and stderr to the null sink\n- cover rotation and service rendering with tests\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --bin lfd daemon_log_keeps_only_one_bounded_predecessor`\n- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 161,
      "deletions": 25,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1050,
      "title": "task: fail closed on committed follow-up past the merged tip",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ops::task\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a worker commits follow-up work on a settled branch after its PR merged, that work belongs to no PR. Previously a completing PR (`after_merge: CompleteTask`) would settle the Task the moment GitHub reported the merge, silently stranding those commits outside the Task. This makes the follow-up boundary an explicit, provable fact: completion is withheld while work sits past the merged tip, and the completing PR — normally terminal — rotates once more so the carry can land on a successor branch.\n\nThe helper that computed the carry range used to collapse \"nothing to carry\" and \"can't tell\" into a single `None`. That's fine for rotation (skip an unsafe cherry-pick) but wrong for completion (an unknown boundary is not an empty one). It now returns a three-way `CommittedFollowUp` — `ProvenEmpty`, `Range`, or `Unprovable` with a reason — so each caller decides for itself.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `committed_follow_up_range` returns `CommittedFollowUp` instead of `Option<(String, String)>`; a missing published head or a head that isn't an ancestor of the branch is `Unprovable`, not empty.\n- The completion gate inspects the newest PR only (rotation leaves the settled branch's commits in place, so scanning every merged PR would never clear) and blocks on a proven range or an unprovable boundary.\n- `Unprovable` does not reverse a Task already `Completed` — missing later evidence blocks entry into completion but can't undo a terminal fact. Repair still reopens on a proven range or any other concrete blocker.\n- Merge reconciliation withholds completion over a committed carry, reading the range from the in-flight PR rather than the store.\n- A completing PR now rotates in exactly one case: a proven `Range` to carry forward.\n- Tests cover rotation-to-carry, both unprovable shapes at the gate, and in-flight merge observation; the `gh` stubs now report a real branch head so fixtures reflect an authoritative merged tip.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 307,
      "deletions": 25,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 1051,
      "title": "task: persist existing headless interaction policy",
      "body": "",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 175,
      "deletions": 2,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 1053,
      "title": "Accounts become the routing primitive",
      "body": "Routes now order provider accounts directly, with a store-wide default for unrouted repositories. Chrome profiles remain only as verified login venues owned by accounts.\n\nThis PR migrates the store to account-first routes and session pins, rekeys forwarded credentials, adds account-addressed route/auth/profile commands, and refuses provider identity mismatches before installing credentials. Automatic runtime selection is SQLite-only; explicit --account selection retains live authentication verification.\n\nThe amended Gate proof runs through connect_account: a drifted Chrome login is skipped with both actual and expected identities rendered, then the next configured venue succeeds. The helper-only attempt path remains deleted. Sabotage control: deleting the expected-login guard makes Profile 3 open, so both the opened-profile assertion and the rendered drift-failure assertion fail; the guard is restored in the published head.\n\nExplicit account identity now composes with foreground SSH independent of repository routing. ForwardedAccountBundle has one required selection authority: routed selections carry a required repository id and routes, while pinned selections carry only concrete provider/account pins. lf --account engineering ssh mini -- lf -m codex : \"work here\" resolves and live-verifies the local account before repository discovery, forwards only its concrete pin and access token, and selects it remotely without a native home, route membership, or automatic health gating. A valid explicit account reaches foreground SSH from outside any repository; missing, ambiguous, empty, and unauthenticated selectors still fail before transport. No-flag SSH retains ordered route failover, and the foreground SSH path removes its temporary store and lease directory on exit.\n\nPR 3 remains intentionally separate: persisted limit-window demotion, route show --explain, and strained-account presentation.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3049,
      "deletions": 2438,
      "changed_files": 19
    },
    {
      "number": 1054,
      "title": "task: wait for green CI before review handoff",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow ci_fix_lifecycle\ncargo test -p loopflow next_move\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAn open PR's next move is now always CI-derived until a fresh required-check reading for the current head proves the checks passed. Previously, a task with an open PR and no CI observation fell straight through to `NextMoveOwner::Review`, so a PR could be handed to review before anyone had seen its checks — including PRs whose checks were red or still running. Now the absence of a reading is itself an answer (\"required checks have not been observed for the current head\"), and CI keeps the move until it can prove otherwise.\n\nThe second half closes the loop on repair: project supervision reconciles its own tasks before touching bodies, so an idle task under a failing head gets a ci-fix wake armed from live control rather than waiting on the body to notice. Task runners consume or retain every ci-fix wake before the shared absorber in `child_control`, which narrows that fallback to the one real case — a child without a bounded repair path already owns the work — and the comment there now says so instead of describing the old birth-time race.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `waves.rs`: `next_move_for_task` returns `Ci` for an open PR with no fresh current-head reading; review requires observed-passing checks\n- `ops/task.rs`: new `reconcile_project_tasks` runs PR/liveness/completion reconciliation and queues ci-fix (or relaunches a parked gate body) ahead of `supervise_project_task_bodies`\n- `task/runner.rs` + `actions.rs`: idle runners arm the ci-fix wake; provider-owned turns retain the claim until the turn ends\n- Attention-projection test fixture now takes a `NextMove` and a CI observation directly, so the gate under test is the real one\n- Expanded `ci_fix_lifecycle_tests` with a parked-gate fixture covering green, failing, and unobserved heads\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1241,
      "deletions": 203,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 1055,
      "title": "release a body lease stuck at `revoked` when its body is provably gone",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow release\ncargo test -p loopflow probe\n```\n\nA Task or Project whose reap failed leaves its body lease pinned at `revoked`. Nothing in the system ever revisited that lease, and the reserve CAS accepts only `NULL` or `finished` — so every future generation was barred forever, and a resume was permanently refused by a corpse. This adds a fail-closed presence probe and releases the lease when the body can be *proven* gone.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe release turns on positive evidence only. `probe_child_body_presence` answers `Absent`, `Present`, or `Unprovable`, and only `Absent` releases; anything else leaves the lease exactly as it was, still doing its job. That third arm is the whole point of not reusing the existing helpers: `tmux_session_exists` maps any non-zero exit to `Ok(false)`, and `process_target_exists` folds `ESRCH` and every unmodelled errno into the same `false`. Both are fail-open, which is harmless for a bounded waiter and wrong for a release — reading an unanswered question as absence would unbar a second body for the Session.\n\nThe probe reads the tmux session first (it carries the `Present` veto, since `observe_provider` overwrites the group with the harness's child group for opencode and codex bodies, while the lf body always runs inside its recorded session), then **one** signal identity — the process group, falling back to the pid only when no group was recorded. Never both: a pid is a recycled resource, so on an old generation the recorded pid may name a stranger, and vetoing on it would pin the lease forever against any future evidence — the defect this exists to clear, rebuilt inside its own fix.\n\nThe release never re-signals. A stuck lease is old by construction, so retrying the kill would mean sending TERM to whatever inherited the pid; and a body proven gone has nothing left to kill.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `engine/process.rs`: `Presence` verdict, `probe_child_body_presence`, and pure classifiers for the errno table and tmux's stderr wordings — including `error connecting to … (No such file or directory)`, which is what tmux 3.6a actually prints when the socket is gone. Missing that wording would have made the feature a no-op on exactly the hosts it exists for: a host whose last body took the server with it reads `Unprovable` on every probe.\n- `ops/child.rs`: `release_dead_revoked_child_body`, plus a reap failure now attempting the release before surfacing the original error.\n- `ops/task.rs` / `ops/project.rs`: launch and strand-recovery release a dead lease before reserving, so recovery doesn't burn its attempt budget on a CAS that cannot pass.\n- A reservation lease-miss now names the lease instead of reporting \"changed from waiting to waiting; retry the command\" — a message that told a machine to retry something that can never pass.\n- `FakeTmux` test fixture pinning `PATH`, so a probe's verdict is a fact about the fixture rather than the host. Errno classification is proven over plain values: a real `EPERM` would be a fact about the CI user (as root none arises at all), and `Group(1)` is no fixture either — it negates to `kill(-1, 0)`, a broadcast over the machine.\n- Kernel-level test: a lease pinned by a dead group releases and only then reserves, with a live unrelated pid recorded as the generation's pid.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 975,
      "deletions": 15,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 1056,
      "title": "task: settle merged work over a proven-empty successor",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task complete W2-304 \"merged work settled\"\ncargo test -p loopflow ops::task\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a Task's PR merges with `after_merge: Review`, the lifecycle rotates a serial successor. If the work is actually done, that successor is never published and never authored — it's an artifact of the rotation, not work. Today `lf task complete` refuses on it, and neither escape hatch is good: publishing opens a zero-commit PR, and abandoning leaves the Session `Waiting` so the next rotation mints PR N+1. This lets completion settle over that successor when — and only when — the branch can be *proven* to hold nothing.\n\nThe proof reuses the merged-follow-up classifier. `commits_past` is factored out of `committed_follow_up_range` so the same tri-state (`ProvenEmpty` / `Range` / `Unprovable`) and the same ancestry rule apply to a new cut: the PR's recorded `base_commit`. `Unprovable` matters because `is_ancestor` maps every nonzero exit to false — a rewritten branch and a missing object arrive identically, and neither proves emptiness. Only `ProvenEmpty`, and only behind a merged predecessor, is discardable.\n\nThe discard is part of completing, not a precondition. `task_completion_gate` classifies without mutating; `task_complete` passes the successor as the completion transaction's `skipped_pr`, so the row is dropped and the terminal status written together. There is no instant where the successor is gone and the Task is not yet terminal — the only state `ensure_working_pr_with_authority` would rotate a fresh empty PR from. The store enforces this too: skipping a PR is legal only while completing.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `commits_past` generalizes the follow-up classifier over an arbitrary cut; `unpublished_work` applies it to a PR's recorded base\n- `CompletionGate` carries `discardable_successor` — classification only, acted on by exactly one caller\n- `PrPhase::Working` blockers now say which of the three cases they hit: empty-with-no-merged-predecessor, committed follow-up, or unprovable\n- `advance_completion_after_gate` declines when a successor is discardable — that path settles through `complete_task_session_after_pr`, which has no `skipped_pr` and would leave a completed Task holding an active PR\n- Three tests prove the behavior: a merged Task settles over an empty successor (and reconcile doesn't walk it back or re-mint), a pending required review blocks while leaving the successor active, and a successor with committed work or an unprovable base is never discardable",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 384,
      "deletions": 23,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 1057,
      "title": "gate: record durable pre-land budget evidence",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/test.py            # run suites the branch touched\nuv run python scripts/test.py --all      # bounded full gate\nuv run python scripts/test.py --history 30  # judge the durable 30-day budget window\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe pre-land gate already printed each phase's `elapsed / budget`, but that evidence died with the terminal — Task-worktree pruning and `.lf/tmp` reaping took it with them, so there was no way to answer \"has the gate stayed inside its budgets?\" over a real observation window. Every invocation now checkpoints a compact JSON record under the repository's Git common directory (`<git-common-dir>/loopflow/pre-land/runs/<kind>/<run-id>.json`), which linked worktrees share and which survives worktree removal, `.lf/tmp` cleanup, and restart. `--history 30` reads that window back and prints an explicit verdict.\n\nThe record holds only operational facts: run identity, branch, commit, timestamps, and per-phase status, elapsed time, and governing budget. Logs, commands, paths, output, diffs, and environment values stay out, and a test pins the schema shut.\n\nBecause `--all` records are the 30-day authority, losing one would bias the evidence — a full run whose record can't be written stops with `MEASUREMENT FAILED` before doing work it couldn't prove. Changed-aware and `--ui-host` runs are diagnostics: they print one `MEASUREMENT WARNING` and preserve their underlying test result.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `_run_command` returns a measured `PhaseOutcome` (budget, elapsed, status, over-budget) instead of an optional failure string; a run is checkpointed as `running` with every phase `not_run` before the first phase starts, so an interrupted gate reads as incomplete rather than absent.\n- Phases after a failure stay visible in the summary and the record as `not_run` with their budget, instead of vanishing.\n- `--history N` is read-only and reports `IN PROGRESS` (clean history younger than the window), `NOT HOLDING` (any over-budget, incomplete, or unreadable full record), or `HOLDING`. A corrupt `full` record is a gap; a corrupt `changed` record is not.\n- `GATE_BUDGET.md` documents the evidence path, the schema boundary, and the verdict rules; a test asserts the document names every executable phase budget, so a budget can't drift away from its published value.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 924,
      "deletions": 46,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 1059,
      "title": "tasks: record a re-minted PR's base from the branch it forks from",
